more internal testing

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:57.496
the numbers become

00:09:57.496 --> 00:09:58.898
not that different

00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:01.734
and then the harder thing becomes

00:10:02.435 --> 00:10:03.736
how you define your problem

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:05.237
define the behavior you want

00:10:05.304 --> 00:10:05.804
mm-hmm

00:10:05.905 --> 00:10:08.040
and when this thing

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.809
isn't defined very clearly

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:13.312
a lot of the model differences actually come from things

00:10:13.412 --> 00:10:15.615
that you wouldn't even imagine

00:10:15.748 --> 00:10:16.248
right

00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:17.717
by 'things you wouldn't imagine,' I mean

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:21.854
I mean, things you wouldn't imagine

00:10:21.854 --> 00:10:23.589
if you ask me now

00:10:23.589 --> 00:10:25.257
it's hard for me to give you a very clear answer

00:10:25.257 --> 00:10:27.259
maybe after some time, looking back

00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:28.260
I'll be able to give a clear answer

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.097
but I can give an example

00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:32.999
of something you wouldn't imagine

00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:36.035
like, if we go back

00:10:36.035 --> 00:10:38.771
maybe one, two, or even three years

00:10:38.771 --> 00:10:41.907
back then, if you went online

00:10:41.974 --> 00:10:44.910
to collect pre-training data

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:48.481
you'd see models learning to write code

00:10:48.481 --> 00:10:50.583
of course, there wasn't this agentic way of writing code back then

00:10:50.583 --> 00:10:51.751
it was just writing a piece of code (mm-hmm)

00:10:51.784 --> 00:10:52.451
and you'd find that

00:10:52.451 --> 00:10:54.420
models wrote code very well

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.288
but back then, people didn't know why

00:10:56.288 --> 00:10:58.424
but the unexpected reason behind this might be

00:10:58.424 --> 00:10:59.658
if you just randomly collect from the web

00:10:59.658 --> 00:11:02.795
without any data filtering

00:11:02.962 --> 00:11:04.363
naturally

00:11:04.363 --> 00:11:06.966
the quality of code data would be a bit higher than others

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:08.701
because if you look at web pages

00:11:08.701 --> 00:11:09.568
you'll find

00:11:09.602 --> 00:11:10.770
GitHub's quality

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.838
is significantly higher than other normal web pages

00:11:13.572 --> 00:11:15.174
before we get into today's topic

00:11:15.174 --> 00:11:15.941
I'd like to talk about

00:11:15.941 --> 00:11:18.310
some recent news about our models

00:11:18.377 --> 00:11:19.512
you see

00:11:19.512 --> 00:11:21.280
everyone's been talking about OpenClaw recently

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:21.981
mm-hmm

00:11:22.214 --> 00:11:23.382
as a frontline researcher

00:11:23.382 --> 00:11:25.418
what do you think of this new product form

00:11:25.418 --> 00:11:26.652
what discussions are happening around you

00:11:27.086 --> 00:11:30.222
what's interesting is

00:11:30.556 --> 00:11:34.760
I feel like the discussion outside the industry

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.796
seems more intense than inside the industry

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:37.997
oh, no one inside the industry is talking about it?

00:11:37.997 --> 00:11:39.231
people inside are talking about it

00:11:39.231 --> 00:11:41.767
but I think for industry insiders

00:11:41.767 --> 00:11:44.003
it's not really, um

00:11:44.003 --> 00:11:45.337
a particularly surprising thing

00:11:45.705 --> 00:11:46.439
oh, what do you mean

00:11:46.439 --> 00:11:48.340
like, maybe inside the company

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:52.545
some people have already done similar experiments

00:11:52.545 --> 00:11:53.646
or demos like this

00:11:53.646 --> 00:11:56.882
it's just that it wasn't packaged as a product

00:11:56.882 --> 00:11:58.084
and seriously marketed

00:11:58.084 --> 00:12:00.119
polished and launched

00:12:00.319 --> 00:12:01.087
right, and

00:12:01.087 --> 00:12:01.887
of course, the reality is

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:03.489
if you look at OpenClaw

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:05.891
the earliest version of the code on GitHub

00:12:05.891 --> 00:12:08.060
actually, that code was

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:08.894
in a sense

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:10.863
not particularly clean

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:12.465
but I think what's important is

00:12:12.465 --> 00:12:15.468
it showed everyone this possibility

00:12:15.468 --> 00:12:17.803
mm-hmm, and after showing this possibility

00:12:17.803 --> 00:12:20.840
the OpenClaw

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:22.241
author himself joined OpenAI

00:12:22.241 --> 00:12:26.112
and then
then probably these model labs

00:12:26.112 --> 00:12:29.115
or some larger startups will catch up quickly

00:12:29.115 --> 00:12:30.883
and polish this into a truly usable product

00:12:31.083 --> 00:12:32.218
mm-hmm (right), so I understand

00:12:32.218 --> 00:12:33.619
actually, before OpenClaw was released

00:12:33.619 --> 00:12:35.421
people at Google were already working on this

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:36.255
it just hadn't been released yet

00:12:36.255 --> 00:12:38.257
because big companies have longer processes

00:12:39.125 --> 00:12:40.726
right, my, my, my

00:12:40.726 --> 00:12:42.561
at least personally

00:12:42.561 --> 00:12:45.064
that's the impression I've gotten

00:12:45.197 --> 00:12:46.332
What we're seeing is exactly that. Right.

00:12:46.432 --> 00:12:49.001
So behind this product form, similar to OpenClaw,

00:12:49.001 --> 00:12:50.202
what does that inherently tell us?

00:12:50.636 --> 00:12:52.338
At this point in early this year,

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:55.007
I think, actually,

00:12:55.541 --> 00:12:58.310
technically speaking,

00:12:58.310 --> 00:12:59.278
it doesn't really prove much.

00:12:59.278 --> 00:13:02.314
I mean, this OpenClaw product,

00:13:02.314 --> 00:13:05.384
of course it relies on many things the model can do,

00:13:05.784 --> 00:13:06.986
but those capabilities

00:13:06.986 --> 00:13:08.821
weren't actually only ready

00:13:08.821 --> 00:13:10.322
by early this year.

00:13:10.556 --> 00:13:11.824
I think maybe last year,

00:13:11.824 --> 00:13:14.527
like when Opus released 4.5 (Claude series),

00:13:14.527 --> 00:13:17.429
and then, and then...

00:13:17.429 --> 00:13:20.266
of course back then, Opus was actually ahead of OpenAI and Gemini 3

00:13:20.266 --> 00:13:22.001
in terms of tool use capabilities.

00:13:22.168 --> 00:13:23.169
So I think at that point,

00:13:23.169 --> 00:13:23.736
doing this thing,

00:13:23.736 --> 00:13:25.838
it was already something you could demonstrate.

00:13:25.971 --> 00:13:27.106
And actually, it

00:13:27.239 --> 00:13:29.608
didn't blow up immediately upon release.

00:13:29.608 --> 00:13:31.944
It only went viral some time after the launch.

00:13:32.278 --> 00:13:32.711
Hmm.

00:13:32.711 --> 00:13:33.779
So,

00:13:33.779 --> 00:13:36.115
for me personally,

00:13:36.115 --> 00:13:38.984
technically it's not really

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:39.818
not really

00:13:39.818 --> 00:13:41.854
something so surprising.

00:13:42.188 --> 00:13:43.656
It's a natural overflow of model capabilities.

00:13:43.656 --> 00:13:45.257
Right, right, right, I'd say so.

00:13:45.257 --> 00:13:45.791
But I think

00:13:45.791 --> 00:13:48.227
the surprise for everyone might be

00:13:48.227 --> 00:13:49.795
that perhaps nobody had realized this before.

00:13:50.362 --> 00:13:51.864
It made everyone realize this could actually be done.

00:13:51.931 --> 00:13:52.631
Realize what?

00:13:52.631 --> 00:13:53.465
Realized that

00:13:53.465 --> 00:13:54.166
you can, like,

00:13:54.166 --> 00:13:55.334
let the model do very...

00:13:55.634 --> 00:13:56.235
I mean,

00:13:56.235 --> 00:13:57.736
you can control many different models

00:13:57.736 --> 00:13:58.737
and do many different things,

00:13:58.737 --> 00:13:59.772
and then aggregate all of that,

00:13:59.772 --> 00:14:00.206
and after aggregating,

00:14:00.206 --> 00:14:02.942
do this kind of very, very, very long-horizon task.

00:14:02.942 --> 00:14:03.542
This kind of work.

00:14:03.876 --> 00:14:04.944
I think maybe

00:14:04.944 --> 00:14:06.679
previously, people hadn't

00:14:06.846 --> 00:14:08.881
widely reached a consensus on this.

00:14:08.881 --> 00:14:09.682
This thing

00:14:10.049 --> 00:14:12.251
showed everyone this kind of possibility.

00:14:13.452 --> 00:14:15.588
You see, what went viral early last year was Manus,

00:14:15.588 --> 00:14:16.922
and what went viral early this year is OpenClaw.

00:14:16.922 --> 00:14:18.123
So from Manus to OpenClaw,

00:14:18.123 --> 00:14:18.958
what changed?

00:14:18.991 --> 00:14:19.925
Is it a change in model capabilities,

00:14:19.925 --> 00:14:20.860
or a change in the product?

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.329
This is also something I've never really understood.

00:14:23.329 --> 00:14:23.696
Hmm.

00:14:23.696 --> 00:14:24.430
Like,

00:14:27.499 --> 00:14:31.604
What is the qualitative difference between Manus and OpenClaw?

00:14:32.037 --> 00:14:32.438
It's something

00:14:32.438 --> 00:14:34.573
I actually haven't quite figured out myself.

00:14:34.707 --> 00:14:36.442
To be honest, haha.

00:14:36.775 --> 00:14:37.109
OK.

00:14:37.109 --> 00:14:38.277
Hmm, like,

00:14:38.277 --> 00:14:39.044
or in other words,

00:14:39.044 --> 00:14:44.650
maybe OpenClaw went viral,

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.719
but if you were to ask me retroactively,

00:14:46.719 --> 00:14:48.487
why Manus couldn't do this,

00:14:49.121 --> 00:14:51.090
I don't understand why Manus couldn't do it.

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:54.193
Maybe they just didn't get it right.

00:14:54.426 --> 00:14:54.994
But you see,

00:14:54.994 --> 00:14:56.328
whether it's Manus or OpenClaw,

00:14:56.328 --> 00:14:57.129
they both chose to sell.

00:14:57.129 --> 00:14:58.397
Manus was sold to Meta (Note: This acquisition has since been revoked; our program was recorded before the revocation).

00:14:58.530 --> 00:14:59.765
OpenClaw was sold to OpenAI.

00:14:59.932 --> 00:15:01.533
What does this phenomenon tell us?

00:15:01.533 --> 00:15:02.768
Why did they both sell?

00:15:03.269 --> 00:15:04.937
I think, hmm,

00:15:04.937 --> 00:15:08.140
my own feeling is that for something

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:11.443
to survive long-term,

00:15:11.443 --> 00:15:13.846
it still needs to have some moats.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:15.381
The moat is the model.

00:15:15.447 --> 00:15:16.715
I think

00:15:16.749 --> 00:15:19.518
at least for now,

00:15:19.518 --> 00:15:21.387
many moats are on the model side.

00:15:21.387 --> 00:15:23.055
But whether product-side moats will emerge in the future,

00:15:23.055 --> 00:15:24.657
I think that's hard to say.

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.358
Because everyone...

00:15:27.159 --> 00:15:29.361
This is all an age-old topic in the market.

00:15:29.361 --> 00:15:31.030
Many people talk about this.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:32.932
Things like data flywheels and such.

00:15:33.065 --> 00:15:34.466
For now,

00:15:34.466 --> 00:15:37.236
I don't think there's any scenario

00:15:37.303 --> 00:15:39.371
that has truly formed a data flywheel.

00:15:39.471 --> 00:15:43.442
Even purely AI-native application scenarios.

00:15:43.442 --> 00:15:45.945
I think currently, besides agentic coding,

00:15:45.945 --> 00:15:47.346
other than writing code,

00:15:47.446 --> 00:15:50.716
there's no scenario that is truly AI-native.

00:15:50.916 --> 00:15:51.917
became hugely successful

00:15:52.351 --> 00:15:54.086
because in a sense

00:15:54.253 --> 00:15:56.288
chatbots are actually an extension of search

00:15:57.723 --> 00:15:59.191
A chatbot is an extension of search

00:15:59.258 --> 00:16:00.826
Right, that's why it's not independent of search

00:16:01.026 --> 00:16:02.695
It's because

00:16:02.695 --> 00:16:03.295
because think about it

00:16:03.295 --> 00:16:08.400
the most common way people interact with chatbots is

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.202
I have a question

00:16:10.202 --> 00:16:11.003
and they ask the chatbot

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:13.806
and that's essentially what search has always done

00:16:13.906 --> 00:16:14.873
But what it offers

00:16:14.873 --> 00:16:17.242
something far better than search is

00:16:17.242 --> 00:16:18.811
it becomes very interactive (交互的)

00:16:18.978 --> 00:16:20.379
It has interactivity

00:16:20.412 --> 00:16:21.480
You can ask follow-up questions

00:16:21.747 --> 00:16:24.350
and it can even help you summarize

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.385
some of the information you get through it

00:16:26.385 --> 00:16:27.453
helping you distill it into

00:16:27.453 --> 00:16:29.855
a condensed answer to your question

00:16:30.189 --> 00:16:32.591
Right, this is something search could never give you before

00:16:32.691 --> 00:16:33.058
Mm-hmm (right)

00:16:33.058 --> 00:16:37.262
But of course it's not exactly the same need

00:16:37.262 --> 00:16:38.864
But in terms of demand

00:16:38.864 --> 00:16:39.965
from a broad demand perspective

00:16:39.965 --> 00:16:41.800
it's fairly similar to the demand that existed before

00:16:42.801 --> 00:16:44.069
Manus and OpenClaw

00:16:44.069 --> 00:16:46.071
I think they're the most famous wrappers right now

00:16:46.505 --> 00:16:48.340
But wrappers ended up being sold to model companies (Note: Meta's acquisition of Manus was later reversed; our show was recorded before the reversal)

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:49.208
Doesn't that show that wrappers

00:16:49.408 --> 00:16:52.044
still can't escape the grip of model companies

00:16:52.544 --> 00:16:53.746
The escape velocity isn't enough

00:16:53.746 --> 00:16:54.880
It's not fast enough, is it

00:16:55.514 --> 00:16:56.582
I think

00:16:58.083 --> 00:17:02.087
I think for wrappers to survive in the current environment

00:17:02.087 --> 00:17:06.592
there are two approaches I can roughly imagine

00:17:06.892 --> 00:17:09.161
One approach is what you just said

00:17:09.161 --> 00:17:10.396
Escape fast enough

00:17:10.963 --> 00:17:13.499
That is, my growth is fast enough

00:17:13.499 --> 00:17:16.502
that by the time model companies catch on

00:17:16.502 --> 00:17:18.971
I've already captured significant user mindshare

00:17:19.505 --> 00:17:23.175
And when model companies catch up to your product

00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:23.709
by that time

00:17:23.709 --> 00:17:25.611
I've already evolved my own model

00:17:25.744 --> 00:17:28.947
I think Cursor is trying to take this path (mm-hmm)

00:17:29.314 --> 00:17:33.185
So Cursor, in this AI-native scenario

00:17:33.185 --> 00:17:34.820
is pretty much the fastest-growing startup

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:35.821
I can think of

00:17:36.555 --> 00:17:37.990
Even a company like that

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:39.491
is feeling a strong sense of crisis right now

00:17:40.058 --> 00:17:40.959
How strong is that sense of crisis

00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:46.098
Anyway, my feeling is that for Cursor

00:17:46.098 --> 00:17:47.800
its relationship with Anthropic right now

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.702
has entered a very delicate phase

00:17:50.803 --> 00:17:51.503
It's like

00:17:51.503 --> 00:17:55.340
They used to be close, seamless partners

00:17:55.474 --> 00:17:56.275
Anthropic provided the model

00:17:56.275 --> 00:17:57.443
Cursor provided the product

00:17:57.576 --> 00:17:59.344
Later Anthropic developed Claude Code itself

00:17:59.711 --> 00:18:01.280
Claude Code has become very successful

00:18:01.513 --> 00:18:03.882
And then
Cursor is now trying to build its own model

00:18:03.882 --> 00:18:06.285
So Cursor is working hard training its Composer

00:18:06.652 --> 00:18:08.053
So

00:18:08.053 --> 00:18:11.190
I don't even think we need to talk about the future

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:11.757
It's already happening right now

00:18:11.757 --> 00:18:14.093
They're already in a fairly competitive relationship (mm-hmm)

00:18:14.093 --> 00:18:17.196
If they lose in this competition

00:18:17.196 --> 00:18:18.464
I think it would be quite problematic

00:18:18.464 --> 00:18:20.599
Because when it comes to coding

00:18:20.599 --> 00:18:21.266
when it comes to coding

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:23.602
at its core it's essentially

00:18:23.602 --> 00:18:24.770
a professional need

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:25.504
serving professional users

00:18:25.504 --> 00:18:26.805
It's a productivity tool

00:18:26.872 --> 00:18:30.509
A common scenario with productivity tools is

00:18:30.943 --> 00:18:31.944
winner takes all

00:18:32.578 --> 00:18:35.781
I think this applies whether to Cursor

00:18:35.781 --> 00:18:36.315
or to Anthropic

00:18:36.315 --> 00:18:38.717
or for any company doing coding

00:18:38.717 --> 00:18:40.352
It's probably something they're all quite worried about

00:18:40.385 --> 00:18:41.587
Mm-hmm (right)

00:18:41.587 --> 00:18:42.855
So that's what I was saying

00:18:42.855 --> 00:18:43.922
That's one path

00:18:43.922 --> 00:18:46.325
(It has to be fast) That is, you grow fast enough

00:18:46.325 --> 00:18:49.928
You grow like crazy before anyone even thinks about acquiring you

00:18:49.928 --> 00:18:50.729
just grow wildly

00:18:50.729 --> 00:18:51.497
By the time they want to acquire you

00:18:51.497 --> 00:18:53.098
you're big enough

00:18:53.265 --> 00:18:55.667
Another way is for the market to be small enough

00:18:56.268 --> 00:18:58.303
so small that model companies can't even be bothered

00:18:58.937 --> 00:19:00.572
I think Midjourney is exactly that example

00:19:01.173 --> 00:19:02.174
That's it

00:19:02.307 --> 00:19:04.109
The market is so small that perhaps

00:19:04.376 --> 00:19:06.578
even though you could say Gemini could make an effort

00:19:06.578 --> 00:19:07.880
to replicate what Midjourney does

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:09.481
It might take some effort

00:19:09.481 --> 00:19:10.115
some money

00:19:10.115 --> 00:19:12.084
some data to pull it off

00:19:12.284 --> 00:19:13.051
but it's small enough

00:19:13.051 --> 00:19:14.119
To the point where

00:19:14.119 --> 00:19:17.322
Gemini probably wouldn't want to spend much time on that

00:19:17.322 --> 00:19:18.123
It's beneath them

00:19:18.757 --> 00:19:20.025
Right, haha

00:19:20.025 --> 00:19:22.728
I think that might also be a way to survive

00:19:22.928 --> 00:19:23.729
Yeah

00:19:24.463 --> 00:19:25.430
So even Cursor

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:29.468
hasn't escaped the model's grasp today

00:19:29.902 --> 00:19:31.370
Has anyone successfully escaped?

00:19:31.937 --> 00:19:35.841
For the big ones, I haven't seen any so far

00:19:35.841 --> 00:19:37.776
For smaller ones, maybe Midjourney is an example

00:19:37.776 --> 00:19:38.644
Of course there must be other examples

00:19:38.644 --> 00:19:39.578
I just haven't seen them yet

00:19:39.578 --> 00:19:40.179
Right, smaller ones

00:19:40.179 --> 00:19:40.779
I think there will be

00:19:40.779 --> 00:19:41.280
There will be examples

00:19:41.813 --> 00:19:42.814
Does Lovart count?

00:19:43.282 --> 00:19:46.485
I think they have a shot

00:19:46.785 --> 00:19:47.753
They have a shot

00:19:49.021 --> 00:19:51.490
Anyway, you can't do the general-purpose thing

00:19:55.127 --> 00:19:58.830
I think this is something the founder has to decide

00:19:59.198 --> 00:20:04.202
Whether you want to bet on something big with a one-in-ten-thousand chance of survival

00:20:04.202 --> 00:20:05.237
and swing for the fences

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:09.074
Or go with a one-percent chance of survival

00:20:09.074 --> 00:20:11.176
and lock down something small first

00:20:11.176 --> 00:20:11.877
If it were you

00:20:11.877 --> 00:20:12.711
What would you choose?

00:20:13.545 --> 00:20:15.914
Hahahaha

00:20:15.981 --> 00:20:16.748
If it were me

00:20:16.748 --> 00:20:18.884
Deep down I'd definitely want to swing for the fences

00:20:18.884 --> 00:20:20.052
But honestly

00:20:20.252 --> 00:20:22.988
I genuinely think

00:20:22.988 --> 00:20:25.657
You can't get there overnight

00:20:26.058 --> 00:20:26.892
So if it were me

00:20:26.892 --> 00:20:28.560
I'd choose to secure a small win first

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:32.197
But I'd pick a small one with huge upside potential

00:20:33.732 --> 00:20:35.901
Why do you think OpenAI acquired OpenClaw?

00:20:35.901 --> 00:20:37.369
Why did Meta want to buy Manus (Note: Meta's acquisition of Manus was later revoked; our show was recorded before the revocation)

00:20:37.836 --> 00:20:39.137
Why doesn't Google acquire anyone?

00:20:39.871 --> 00:20:40.772
Oh, Google did acquire someone

00:20:40.772 --> 00:20:42.774
Google bought the Windsurf team

00:20:42.975 --> 00:20:44.176
Okay, Windsurf

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:45.611
Yeah

00:20:46.979 --> 00:20:47.546
I don't get it

00:20:47.546 --> 00:20:48.914
Haha

00:20:49.081 --> 00:20:49.915
What do you mean you don't get it?

00:20:50.015 --> 00:20:51.183
Honestly, it's just that

00:20:51.183 --> 00:20:52.217
I don't get it

00:20:52.584 --> 00:20:54.186
I think

00:20:55.487 --> 00:20:59.891
I think Meta's acquisition of Manus

00:21:00.225 --> 00:21:01.860
I think for them

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:04.096
The biggest benefit was

00:21:04.096 --> 00:21:04.796
If

00:21:04.796 --> 00:21:06.498
aside from how much they spent

00:21:06.598 --> 00:21:09.101
The biggest benefit was gaining a really strong

00:21:09.101 --> 00:21:11.336
product team in Asia

00:21:12.237 --> 00:21:13.472
What does being in Asia signify?

00:21:13.472 --> 00:21:14.640
Because

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:16.074
I think on one hand

00:21:17.175 --> 00:21:19.378
Obviously everyone knows

00:21:19.378 --> 00:21:22.314
China's AI talent pool is still quite deep

00:21:22.614 --> 00:21:25.183
Although perhaps currently in terms of technology

00:21:25.183 --> 00:21:26.618
Purely from a technical standpoint

00:21:26.618 --> 00:21:29.888
Chinese AI hasn't really caught up with the US yet

00:21:30.122 --> 00:21:31.523
But

00:21:31.523 --> 00:21:33.692
Obviously there are many talented AI people in China

00:21:33.692 --> 00:21:36.194
Whether in pure technology or in product

00:21:36.194 --> 00:21:39.031
In terms of product, I think China essentially

00:21:39.031 --> 00:21:40.465
has better talent than the US

00:21:40.799 --> 00:21:42.067
Right, so for them

00:21:42.067 --> 00:21:44.069
I think Manus became a

00:21:44.069 --> 00:21:45.637
foothold in Singapore

00:21:46.238 --> 00:21:48.073
So they can attract some

00:21:48.073 --> 00:21:48.907
For example, from China

00:21:48.907 --> 00:21:51.576
Or from Singapore or East Asian talent

00:21:52.044 --> 00:21:58.216
And I actually haven't fully figured out

00:21:58.617 --> 00:22:02.854
How important this product itself is to Meta

00:22:02.888 --> 00:22:03.622
Or in other words

00:22:03.622 --> 00:22:05.424
Why couldn't Meta just build this product themselves?

00:22:05.824 --> 00:22:07.859
But whether it's Manus or OpenClaw

00:22:07.859 --> 00:22:10.195
They were in fact born from outside teams

00:22:10.195 --> 00:22:10.562
Why

00:22:10.562 --> 00:22:13.231
weren't they built by this group of Silicon Valley researchers?

00:22:13.398 --> 00:22:14.633
Have you thought about that?

00:22:14.866 --> 00:22:16.134
Yeah, I think

00:22:16.134 --> 00:22:17.636
Hmm, for me this question

00:22:17.636 --> 00:22:19.404
Actually

00:22:20.005 --> 00:22:23.475
I think once a company gets big

00:22:23.475 --> 00:22:24.843
Its burden gets bigger too

00:22:25.477 --> 00:22:30.015
Like, I might be a researcher

00:22:30.015 --> 00:22:33.585
and we can build something really

00:22:33.585 --> 00:22:34.386
interesting-looking

00:22:34.386 --> 00:22:36.455
very distinctive products

00:22:36.555 --> 00:22:39.725
But once I make that product public

00:22:39.725 --> 00:22:41.560
There's a ton of responsibility that comes with it

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:43.729
First, you can't just launch this product

00:22:43.729 --> 00:22:46.398
and tell all your users

00:22:46.798 --> 00:22:48.600
You need to go buy another computer to do this

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:50.736
Otherwise it might gain access to everything on your computer

00:22:50.736 --> 00:22:51.370
All the permissions—

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:52.104
and crash your system

00:22:52.104 --> 00:22:52.704
Mm-hmm

00:22:52.771 --> 00:22:54.072
So for a big company

00:22:54.072 --> 00:22:54.573
Take Google, for example

00:22:54.573 --> 00:22:56.274
Google would never release a product like this

00:22:56.274 --> 00:22:57.042
Right? Mm-hmm

00:22:57.042 --> 00:22:59.611
So it takes a lot of time to polish the product

00:22:59.611 --> 00:23:01.213
And you have to make sure

00:23:01.213 --> 00:23:03.382
there are no legal risks

00:23:03.382 --> 00:23:07.052
and that it won't damage your brand with users

00:23:07.052 --> 00:23:08.420
Plus,

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:10.455
if you ship it

00:23:10.455 --> 00:23:12.157
you probably have to allocate

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:13.825
some relatively fixed resources

00:23:13.825 --> 00:23:15.827
to serve this model

00:23:15.827 --> 00:23:17.429
or serve this

00:23:17.462 --> 00:23:18.363
product line

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:19.731
So yeah, yeah

00:23:19.731 --> 00:23:20.399
For big companies

00:23:20.399 --> 00:23:22.401
I think there's quite a lot of burden

00:23:22.601 --> 00:23:23.468
But for individuals

00:23:23.468 --> 00:23:24.035
it doesn't matter

00:23:24.035 --> 00:23:26.571
I mean, it's an open-source project anyway

00:23:26.772 --> 00:23:28.240
So what if my code is terrible

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:29.374
Come help me write it

00:23:29.374 --> 00:23:30.909
Right? Hahaha, yeah

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.278
I think whether it's Manus or OpenClaw

00:23:33.278 --> 00:23:34.679
they actually point to a direction

00:23:34.746 --> 00:23:35.881
which is

00:23:35.881 --> 00:23:38.416
this is also a possible narrative for 2026

00:23:38.717 --> 00:23:39.484
What are your thoughts on 2026

00:23:39.684 --> 00:23:41.653
and what are your expectations

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:46.958
I think there are really so many possibilities

00:23:46.958 --> 00:23:48.627
And for me

00:23:48.627 --> 00:23:51.897
in terms of model capabilities

00:23:52.164 --> 00:23:54.132
I think

00:23:54.132 --> 00:23:57.235
Models— I sometimes really love saying this slogan

00:23:57.335 --> 00:23:59.171
which is that models should achieve

00:23:59.337 --> 00:24:03.608
train with finite context, use as infinite context (finite in training, infinite in use)

00:24:03.608 --> 00:24:04.142
In other words

00:24:04.142 --> 00:24:06.645
you use this limited

00:24:06.645 --> 00:24:08.513
this context length (context window) to train it

00:24:08.513 --> 00:24:10.649
but in usage, it can use a very, very long

00:24:10.649 --> 00:24:12.751
even nearly infinite context length

00:24:12.751 --> 00:24:13.752
I think this

00:24:14.019 --> 00:24:17.689
has a chance of being realized this year

00:24:17.856 --> 00:24:19.057
And once this is achieved

00:24:19.057 --> 00:24:22.294
I think it will unlock many new applications

00:24:22.294 --> 00:24:24.763
because, to give the simplest example

00:24:24.763 --> 00:24:27.232
you could potentially let this model

00:24:27.232 --> 00:24:28.467
interact with you continuously

00:24:28.467 --> 00:24:30.202
and continuously receive your information

00:24:30.335 --> 00:24:32.337
And as it runs

00:24:32.337 --> 00:24:35.040
it will continuously evaluate the current context and your conversation

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:37.709
and possibly discard information it deems unimportant

00:24:37.843 --> 00:24:40.278
And then it becomes

00:24:40.278 --> 00:24:42.047
the personal assistant everyone dreams of

00:24:42.647 --> 00:24:44.316
Yeah, I think technically speaking

00:24:44.316 --> 00:24:45.483
I think this will

00:24:45.483 --> 00:24:47.486
definitely be realized this year no matter what

00:24:47.486 --> 00:24:48.420
But of course, of course

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:52.090
I think what people haven't reached consensus on yet is

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:53.825
how to technically achieve this

00:24:53.925 --> 00:24:54.492
Mm-hmm

00:24:54.559 --> 00:24:56.428
Obviously there are many technical paths

00:24:56.428 --> 00:24:58.763
But I think right now it's more about

00:24:58.763 --> 00:25:03.268
trying to see which path can work

00:25:03.501 --> 00:25:05.270
There might be several paths that all work

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:08.173
Then we'll have to test them experimentally

00:25:08.173 --> 00:25:11.276
under common user scenarios

00:25:11.276 --> 00:25:13.378
to see which path is the most efficient

00:25:13.578 --> 00:25:15.981
Yeah, I think we're more at this stage right now

00:25:15.981 --> 00:25:19.384
rather than a stage where no one has ideas

00:25:19.384 --> 00:25:20.085
Everyone has ideas

00:25:20.085 --> 00:25:22.220
but we need to figure out which idea is the right one

00:25:22.988 --> 00:25:25.390
Standing here in Q1 2026 as

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:26.591
a frontline researcher

00:25:26.591 --> 00:25:29.060
do you think the pace of model improvement is slowing down

00:25:29.895 --> 00:25:32.597
I think not at all (not at all) I think not at all

00:25:32.597 --> 00:25:34.966
How does its velocity curve compare to '25

00:25:34.966 --> 00:25:37.435
and what's changed from '24

00:25:38.403 --> 00:25:40.538
Mm, it's hard to say quantitatively

00:25:40.538 --> 00:25:43.108
because you need to give me a standard

00:25:43.141 --> 00:25:44.509
before I can quantitatively tell you

00:25:44.676 --> 00:25:46.545
because if the standard you give is, like

00:25:46.545 --> 00:25:48.280
I just look at some Benchmark

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:49.481
like, say, SWE-bench

00:25:49.481 --> 00:25:51.416
how many points it gains each month

00:25:51.549 --> 00:25:53.084
then this will definitely slow down

00:25:53.585 --> 00:25:55.020
because by definition

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:56.588
this Benchmark maxes out at 100%

00:25:56.588 --> 00:25:57.088
Mm-hmm

00:25:57.088 --> 00:25:57.722
so the closer you get

00:25:57.722 --> 00:25:59.090
the slower it definitely gets

00:25:59.457 --> 00:26:00.992
but this doesn't necessarily mean

00:26:00.992 --> 00:26:03.828
that users feel the model's capability growth has slowed

00:26:03.828 --> 00:26:06.865
because going from 50% to 60%

00:26:06.865 --> 00:26:07.632
it might feel like, hey

00:26:07.632 --> 00:26:08.366
that's a bit better

00:26:08.366 --> 00:26:09.000
but quite possibly

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:11.469
For example, from 70% to 75%

00:26:11.536 --> 00:26:13.438
It found that the gains are even greater than from 50% to 60%

00:26:13.705 --> 00:26:14.105
Mm-hmm

00:26:14.105 --> 00:26:14.940
That's entirely possible

00:26:15.006 --> 00:26:16.374
If it's from 80% to 90%

00:26:16.374 --> 00:26:18.944
Or 90% to 100%, the difference would feel even more significant

00:26:19.044 --> 00:26:20.245
Not necessarily

00:26:20.245 --> 00:26:21.446
Because maybe past

00:26:21.446 --> 00:26:23.048
Maybe around 80% to 90%

00:26:23.048 --> 00:26:24.583
Users wouldn't notice any difference

00:26:24.583 --> 00:26:25.550
It might even get worse

00:26:25.684 --> 00:26:26.785
You said it doesn't get slower at all

00:26:26.785 --> 00:26:27.786
Based on what criteria?

00:26:27.819 --> 00:26:29.788
I think it's based on

00:26:29.788 --> 00:26:32.290
my personal feeling as a researcher

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:32.924
Like

00:26:32.924 --> 00:26:34.659
My personal impression is

00:26:34.659 --> 00:26:38.263
the model's ability to learn things is getting stronger and stronger

00:26:38.296 --> 00:26:39.731
It used to take a lot of effort

00:26:39.731 --> 00:26:43.068
to get the model to learn to do something

00:26:43.435 --> 00:26:45.437
But now it probably doesn't require that much effort

00:26:45.437 --> 00:26:46.071
The most important thing is

00:26:46.071 --> 00:26:47.806
you need to clearly define the problem

00:26:47.906 --> 00:26:51.409
and figure out how to build the right data (Mm-hmm)

00:26:51.509 --> 00:26:52.110
Of course, data

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:53.378
Data is broader now

00:26:53.378 --> 00:26:56.181
including environments and such

00:26:56.448 --> 00:26:58.083
And

00:26:58.083 --> 00:26:59.050
the rest

00:26:59.050 --> 00:27:03.054
often seems to fall into place naturally

00:27:03.221 --> 00:27:03.721
Right

00:27:03.922 --> 00:27:05.290
Why is the learning ability getting stronger?

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:06.958
The model's learning ability has improved

00:27:07.225 --> 00:27:09.828
I think maybe on one hand

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:12.797
There could be many reasons

00:27:12.797 --> 00:27:15.767
But I think one reason is pre-training

00:27:15.767 --> 00:27:17.602
Actually, over the past few months

00:27:17.602 --> 00:27:19.237
I think it has been getting stronger

00:27:19.304 --> 00:27:19.804
Pre-training

00:27:19.838 --> 00:27:20.805
Right, right

00:27:20.805 --> 00:27:21.473
Model pre-training

00:27:21.473 --> 00:27:23.341
has actually gotten stronger in the past few months (Mm-hmm)

00:27:23.508 --> 00:27:25.944
I think this might be

00:27:26.811 --> 00:27:28.646
somewhat controversial in a sense

00:27:28.646 --> 00:27:30.315
Because a few months ago

00:27:30.315 --> 00:27:31.349
I think

00:27:31.349 --> 00:27:34.019
many people were already discussing whether

00:27:34.019 --> 00:27:34.719
this Scaling Law

00:27:34.719 --> 00:27:35.754
had reached its limit

00:27:35.887 --> 00:27:36.521
Mm-hmm

00:27:37.689 --> 00:27:38.890
My experience is that it hasn't

00:27:39.591 --> 00:27:40.592
And my feeling is

00:27:40.592 --> 00:27:43.228
in the next four months

00:27:43.528 --> 00:27:45.296
I don't see any signs of it ending either

00:27:46.731 --> 00:27:47.699
Mm-hmm

00:27:48.033 --> 00:27:49.401
Why do people think it's reaching its limit?

00:27:49.734 --> 00:27:51.202
I think, well

00:27:51.202 --> 00:27:51.836
I-I-I

00:27:51.836 --> 00:27:53.271
obviously don't know

00:27:53.271 --> 00:27:54.873
why people think it's reached its limit

00:27:54.873 --> 00:27:56.341
Because I myself don't feel it's reached its limit

00:27:56.341 --> 00:27:59.210
But my guess would be

00:28:00.278 --> 00:28:02.914
When someone thinks a pattern has reached its limit

00:28:03.815 --> 00:28:04.416
it's basically

00:28:04.849 --> 00:28:06.117
one of these two situations

00:28:06.117 --> 00:28:06.617
Ah

00:28:07.452 --> 00:28:08.653
One situation is

00:28:08.653 --> 00:28:11.790
they feel the applicable range of this pattern has reached its limit

00:28:12.157 --> 00:28:13.324
Ah, maybe

00:28:13.458 --> 00:28:14.759
Maybe

00:28:14.759 --> 00:28:15.794
Fundamentally speaking

00:28:15.794 --> 00:28:16.261
Scaling Law

00:28:16.261 --> 00:28:17.962
simply can't extend infinitely

00:28:18.329 --> 00:28:19.831
which could be true

00:28:19.831 --> 00:28:21.199
But this is just a guess

00:28:21.199 --> 00:28:22.600
That is, this person might feel that

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:25.003
the applicable range of this pattern has reached its limit

00:28:25.036 --> 00:28:25.970
Another possibility is

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:26.971
this person feels that

00:28:27.405 --> 00:28:28.306
this pattern

00:28:28.306 --> 00:28:30.341
one of its conditions can no longer be met

00:28:30.742 --> 00:28:35.013
For example, they feel that data has already hit a wall

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:37.248
Then I simply haven't extended it further

00:28:37.782 --> 00:28:38.650
Another possibility

00:28:38.917 --> 00:28:40.652
But actually there's a third possibility

00:28:41.052 --> 00:28:43.354
The third possibility is that

00:28:44.355 --> 00:28:46.491
there's a bug somewhere in their work

00:28:46.958 --> 00:28:48.159
that they haven't noticed themselves

00:28:48.526 --> 00:28:49.728
So they think it's reached its limit

00:28:49.861 --> 00:28:51.062
Oh

00:28:51.229 --> 00:28:53.898
From my perspective

00:28:53.898 --> 00:28:55.366
From my observation

00:28:55.366 --> 00:28:57.068
I think

00:28:59.337 --> 00:29:01.573
probably the vast majority of people who hit a wall

00:29:01.573 --> 00:29:02.474
it's because of the third reason

00:29:03.108 --> 00:29:03.975
It's because there's a bug

00:29:04.309 --> 00:29:05.210
What kind of bug?

00:29:05.477 --> 00:29:06.678
I think

00:29:06.678 --> 00:29:08.346
There are many possible kinds of bugs

00:29:08.346 --> 00:29:10.348
For example, one possibility is

00:29:10.348 --> 00:29:12.183
When you're working on Scaling Laws

00:29:12.183 --> 00:29:14.152
Some scientific assumptions weren't quite right

00:29:14.319 --> 00:29:16.955
For example, what kind of token horizon you choose

00:29:16.955 --> 00:29:19.090
That is, for each model size, what kind of

00:29:19.090 --> 00:29:21.793
expected training data volume you pick

00:29:21.793 --> 00:29:23.294
And then this amount of data

00:29:24.529 --> 00:29:25.930
Where this data comes from

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:27.332
And then

00:29:27.332 --> 00:29:30.101
It's possible that these more scientific choices

00:29:30.101 --> 00:29:30.702
weren't made clearly

00:29:30.702 --> 00:29:31.669
That's one possibility

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:33.605
But I think there's another possibility, which is

00:29:33.605 --> 00:29:34.606
there's simply a bug

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:38.810
Actually, I don't think this is surprising in the industry

00:29:39.077 --> 00:29:40.612
Many times

00:29:41.412 --> 00:29:42.347
Fixing a single bug

00:29:42.347 --> 00:29:43.281
The progress it brings

00:29:43.281 --> 00:29:47.352
is far greater than some fancy tricks

00:29:47.819 --> 00:29:48.319
Right

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:52.790
And of course, there are other situations

00:29:52.790 --> 00:29:54.826
These two examples I just gave

00:29:54.826 --> 00:29:57.262
are situations I've seen quite often

00:29:57.729 --> 00:29:58.897
So how do you deal with bugs

00:29:58.897 --> 00:30:00.398
How do you solve bug problems

00:30:01.132 --> 00:30:01.966
I think, right

00:30:01.966 --> 00:30:03.701
I feel like this is more of a mindset issue

00:30:03.701 --> 00:30:04.936
Because when you encounter a bug

00:30:04.936 --> 00:30:06.104
If you think it can't be fixed

00:30:06.104 --> 00:30:07.505
You'll say we've hit a wall

00:30:07.539 --> 00:30:08.173
When you encounter a bug

00:30:08.173 --> 00:30:08.973
I think, oh

00:30:08.973 --> 00:30:10.475
This can definitely be fixed

00:30:10.742 --> 00:30:12.243
Then you'll feel like we haven't hit a wall yet

00:30:12.343 --> 00:30:13.978
Because everyone definitely encounters bugs

00:30:14.846 --> 00:30:16.981
I think, I think

00:30:17.148 --> 00:30:18.550
This might be like what you said

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:21.186
That is
There are some things that are more about belief

00:30:21.186 --> 00:30:22.086
But for me

00:30:22.086 --> 00:30:24.055
A more important thing is the working system

00:30:24.956 --> 00:30:29.227
That is, when something

00:30:30.094 --> 00:30:31.996
is different from what you predicted

00:30:31.996 --> 00:30:34.799
Can you systematically rule out various possibilities

00:30:34.966 --> 00:30:36.868
I think this is a very important thing

00:30:37.068 --> 00:30:37.669
Mmhmm

00:30:38.336 --> 00:30:41.372
This
is something I think Gemini and Anthropic do well

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:42.774
That is

00:30:43.441 --> 00:30:44.442
Especially in pre-training

00:30:44.442 --> 00:30:48.379
That is, when behavior at a certain scale

00:30:48.379 --> 00:30:50.548
might be different from what you imagined

00:30:50.615 --> 00:30:53.785
People can design reasonable

00:30:53.818 --> 00:30:55.086
what we call ablation experiments (消融实验)

00:30:55.086 --> 00:30:56.254
reasonable experiments like this

00:30:56.254 --> 00:30:57.288
can help you see

00:30:57.288 --> 00:30:58.856
test whether some of your

00:30:58.856 --> 00:31:00.725
imagined possible factors

00:31:00.725 --> 00:31:01.926
are actually the real factors

00:31:02.327 --> 00:31:03.394
I think this

00:31:03.394 --> 00:31:06.731
systematic approach to problem-solving is the key

00:31:07.065 --> 00:31:07.832
Mmhmm

00:31:09.033 --> 00:31:10.435
You think

00:31:10.435 --> 00:31:12.136
Model capabilities can still improve

00:31:12.136 --> 00:31:13.171
Then its driving force

00:31:13.171 --> 00:31:13.738
Data and compute

00:31:13.738 --> 00:31:14.138
Algorithms

00:31:14.138 --> 00:31:15.940
Which do you think is the main driving force

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:20.511
I think they all contribute

00:31:20.511 --> 00:31:24.148
But in a sense

00:31:24.148 --> 00:31:25.884
Data and compute are two things

00:31:25.884 --> 00:31:28.486
that are actually very strongly correlated

00:31:28.486 --> 00:31:29.454
Data and compute, mmhmm

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:30.755
Right, because

00:31:30.755 --> 00:31:31.489
When your compute goes up

00:31:31.489 --> 00:31:32.857
you'll naturally attract more data

00:31:32.857 --> 00:31:33.524
When data goes up

00:31:33.524 --> 00:31:34.959
you'll naturally need more compute

00:31:34.959 --> 00:31:36.527
Right, and then

00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:38.630
For algorithms, I think

00:31:39.631 --> 00:31:43.768
Algorithmic progress often has a phase transition

00:31:43.968 --> 00:31:46.070
That is, there's a phase

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:49.173
where you haven't figured out what to do at all

00:31:49.474 --> 00:31:51.709
At that stage, algorithms are extremely critical

00:31:52.043 --> 00:31:54.178
Because when you haven't figured out what to do at all

00:31:54.178 --> 00:31:56.114
you might have no way to scale up at all

00:31:56.481 --> 00:31:57.715
And then you might get stuck there

00:31:57.949 --> 00:31:58.983
But at a certain point

00:31:58.983 --> 00:32:00.018
you might discover

00:32:00.018 --> 00:32:01.419
the most important thing in the algorithm

00:32:01.819 --> 00:32:03.021
Then it might suddenly go from

00:32:03.021 --> 00:32:04.889
completely impossible to possible

00:32:04.956 --> 00:32:06.124
And then after that, algorithmic improvements

00:32:06.124 --> 00:32:08.526
are more of a gradual improvement

00:32:08.526 --> 00:32:11.362
That is
It might improve your computational efficiency

00:32:11.362 --> 00:32:12.764
or the efficiency of using data

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:15.166
Right, and then

00:32:15.199 --> 00:32:16.501
Let me give an example

00:32:16.501 --> 00:32:21.906
For example, from the perspective of language model pre-training

00:32:22.206 --> 00:32:24.942
Then this leap in algorithms

00:32:24.942 --> 00:32:25.910
Well

00:32:25.943 --> 00:32:27.645
I mean, the development of the Transformer

00:32:28.079 --> 00:32:30.515
But after the Transformer was discovered

00:32:30.648 --> 00:32:32.984
It's been mostly gradual and smooth

00:32:32.984 --> 00:32:34.085
Improving its efficiency

00:32:34.085 --> 00:32:35.320
Or your use of data

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:37.388
Or the efficiency of compute usage has been improving

00:32:37.388 --> 00:32:38.189
Right

00:32:38.756 --> 00:32:41.392
So the current drivers are compute and data

00:32:41.893 --> 00:32:46.464
I think within the relatively clear frameworks we have now

00:32:46.664 --> 00:32:48.599
The main drivers are compute and data

00:32:48.599 --> 00:32:49.434
By clear framework, I mean

00:32:49.434 --> 00:32:51.035
For example, pre-training and post-training

00:32:51.035 --> 00:32:53.838
Whether it's post-training based on reinforcement learning

00:32:53.838 --> 00:32:56.341
Or based on supervised learning

00:32:56.341 --> 00:32:57.642
That is, post-training with supervised learning

00:32:57.875 --> 00:33:00.445
For example, within these two relatively clear

00:33:00.578 --> 00:33:02.580
paradigms (范式)

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:06.084
Indeed, compute and data are the main drivers

00:33:06.184 --> 00:33:07.752
But it's undeniable

00:33:07.752 --> 00:33:09.988
That in some other directions, the driving factors might be different

00:33:10.788 --> 00:33:11.756
Hmm, what do you mean?

00:33:11.756 --> 00:33:12.523
To give a simple example

00:33:12.523 --> 00:33:13.891
For instance, multimodal generation

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:15.760
Hmm

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:18.463
Well
I think it's probably something that, algorithmically speaking

00:33:18.896 --> 00:33:20.098
Hasn't been fully figured out yet

00:33:21.332 --> 00:33:22.600
So that's still a scientific problem

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:23.368
That hasn't been solved yet

00:33:23.568 --> 00:33:24.068
Right

00:33:25.536 --> 00:33:28.006
But language is no longer a scientific problem

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:31.309
Natural language generation

00:33:31.309 --> 00:33:32.477
I think, for now

00:33:32.477 --> 00:33:34.679
Before this technical approach hits a wall

00:33:34.679 --> 00:33:37.682
I think it's relatively clear scientifically

00:33:37.915 --> 00:33:38.549
But in terms of engineering

00:33:38.549 --> 00:33:40.418
There's still so, so, so much to be done

00:33:41.719 --> 00:33:42.987
How much more do you think pre-training can improve?

00:33:43.354 --> 00:33:44.956
Improving model capabilities through pre-training

00:33:44.956 --> 00:33:45.723
How much more

00:33:45.723 --> 00:33:46.858
How much further can it go

00:33:46.858 --> 00:33:47.492
Can we expect

00:33:49.761 --> 00:33:50.428
That's just how people are

00:33:50.428 --> 00:33:52.330
I mean, when you haven't hit the wall, you

00:33:52.330 --> 00:33:53.731
Don't actually know how long the road is

00:33:54.031 --> 00:33:54.966
What I can

00:33:54.966 --> 00:33:57.101
What I can see is that we haven't hit the wall yet

00:33:57.368 --> 00:33:58.903
But I don't know when we'll hit it either

00:33:59.303 --> 00:34:01.305
If I really had to estimate a timeline

00:34:01.305 --> 00:34:01.806
As I just said

00:34:01.806 --> 00:34:02.840
I think four months

00:34:03.174 --> 00:34:05.610
The next four months will still see progress

00:34:06.044 --> 00:34:07.345
But in the AI field

00:34:07.345 --> 00:34:09.147
No one can predict what happens after four months

00:34:09.180 --> 00:34:10.748
Hmm, so over the past few months

00:34:10.748 --> 00:34:12.784
When you look at pre-training and model capabilities

00:34:12.784 --> 00:34:13.918
You're still very excited

00:34:14.852 --> 00:34:17.455
Is this the general mindset and state around you?

00:34:18.756 --> 00:34:20.024
I think so

00:34:20.324 --> 00:34:21.025
I think so

00:34:21.092 --> 00:34:22.560
Is this within a small environment at Google

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:24.028
Or in the entire Silicon Valley environment

00:34:24.562 --> 00:34:25.863
I think it's hard to say for all of Silicon Valley

00:34:25.863 --> 00:34:27.265
Because Silicon Valley is too big a place

00:34:27.565 --> 00:34:29.000
People working on products might be excited about products

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:29.767
Right, for product people

00:34:29.767 --> 00:34:31.569
What excites them most might be something like OpenClaw

00:34:32.203 --> 00:34:32.637
Hmm

00:34:32.637 --> 00:34:34.505
But for people working on models

00:34:34.505 --> 00:34:34.906
It's probably

00:34:34.906 --> 00:34:37.475
That we get more excited about this kind of model progress

00:34:37.475 --> 00:34:38.176
Hmm

00:34:38.176 --> 00:34:39.043
I think

00:34:39.043 --> 00:34:39.911
Uh

00:34:41.079 --> 00:34:42.613
For people working on models

00:34:42.613 --> 00:34:44.248
Is excitement a consensus?

00:34:44.248 --> 00:34:44.849
Over the past four months

00:34:44.882 --> 00:34:45.950
I personally think so

00:34:46.317 --> 00:34:47.585
Oh, I personally think so

00:34:48.086 --> 00:34:50.421
At least within the circle I have access to

00:34:50.521 --> 00:34:51.556
I think at

00:34:51.556 --> 00:34:53.024
Anthropic and Google, people

00:34:53.124 --> 00:34:56.027
Or at Gemini, people are probably thinking more about

00:34:56.627 --> 00:34:59.430
How our AI will keep progressing

00:34:59.597 --> 00:35:01.299
And soon we'll be replaced

00:35:01.299 --> 00:35:03.301
After being replaced, what should we do?

00:35:03.301 --> 00:35:06.704
Haha, rather than worrying about what to do when models hit a wall

00:35:06.704 --> 00:35:07.505
Hahaha

00:35:08.539 --> 00:35:09.340
Speaking of which

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:10.675
Why

00:35:10.675 --> 00:35:11.976
Over the past few months

00:35:11.976 --> 00:35:14.078
Coding has been developing the fastest

00:35:14.745 --> 00:35:16.180
Why is this the case?

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:18.282
I think the coding scenario

00:35:18.616 --> 00:35:20.651
First of all, coding itself

00:35:20.651 --> 00:35:22.720
Hasn't just been developing the fastest over the past few months

00:35:23.020 --> 00:35:24.255
I think coding itself

00:35:24.255 --> 00:35:26.891
Actually

00:35:27.291 --> 00:35:29.527
From Claude 3.5 (new)

00:35:29.727 --> 00:35:32.063
Or some people out there called it Claude 3.6 (yeah)

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:32.930
After that

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:34.799
It's been in a state of rapid development ever since

00:35:35.666 --> 00:35:36.334
And I think

00:35:36.334 --> 00:35:37.735
That was early last year

00:35:37.735 --> 00:35:38.769
Or the end of the year before

00:35:38.769 --> 00:35:42.406
That was October of the year before last

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:43.141
October of the year before last

00:35:43.207 --> 00:35:44.041
Yeah, yeah

00:35:44.842 --> 00:35:47.478
It should be, maybe October or November

00:35:47.478 --> 00:35:48.412
But around that time

00:35:49.413 --> 00:35:49.881
From then on

00:35:49.881 --> 00:35:51.516
I've been in a state of rapid development

00:35:51.849 --> 00:35:54.986
I think the coding scenario has

00:35:55.319 --> 00:35:57.021
Two biggest advantages

00:35:57.722 --> 00:36:01.993
The first advantage is its reward signal (回馈信号)

00:36:01.993 --> 00:36:04.462
That is, its feedback signal

00:36:04.762 --> 00:36:07.031
Is very well-defined

00:36:07.598 --> 00:36:09.400
Because

00:36:10.601 --> 00:36:11.802
For example, if you

00:36:11.802 --> 00:36:14.572
For example, something like a software engineer (软件工程师) task

00:36:14.605 --> 00:36:16.374
Often the situation is

00:36:16.374 --> 00:36:18.476
I need to write some code

00:36:18.776 --> 00:36:20.177
To implement a feature

00:36:20.177 --> 00:36:21.412
A feature

00:36:21.445 --> 00:36:24.815
(Yeah) This feature needs certain inputs

00:36:24.815 --> 00:36:26.017
And produces certain outputs

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:28.119
This is something very easy to

00:36:28.352 --> 00:36:30.388
Very easy to test

00:36:30.621 --> 00:36:32.557
So its feedback signal is very clear

00:36:32.557 --> 00:36:35.760
Your input and output match up

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:38.763
Then it means your implementation is successful

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:40.431
If not, then it's unsuccessful (Yeah)

00:36:40.531 --> 00:36:41.866
But this is just one example

00:36:41.866 --> 00:36:44.235
In coding-related work

00:36:44.235 --> 00:36:45.036
There are many, many

00:36:45.036 --> 00:36:47.405
Many such well-defined feedback signals

00:36:47.538 --> 00:36:51.142
And another big advantage is

00:36:52.343 --> 00:36:56.614
Coding data has a very natural foundation

00:36:57.381 --> 00:36:58.349
That foundation is GitHub

00:36:59.283 --> 00:37:03.521
GitHub has aggregated over the past few, roughly

00:37:03.521 --> 00:37:04.722
Decades

00:37:05.990 --> 00:37:09.760
A lot of high-quality code written by many excellent programmers

00:37:10.094 --> 00:37:11.462
And starting from that code

00:37:11.462 --> 00:37:14.198
You can build a tremendous number of environments

00:37:15.066 --> 00:37:18.169
I think these two things, from a model perspective

00:37:18.169 --> 00:37:20.471
Are why coding can be done very well

00:37:20.738 --> 00:37:23.274
Of course, I think from a product perspective

00:37:23.307 --> 00:37:24.942
There's another reason

00:37:24.942 --> 00:37:27.278
Which is that coding

00:37:27.478 --> 00:37:29.614
The demand for this product

00:37:29.614 --> 00:37:31.549
Is in a sense

00:37:31.549 --> 00:37:33.084
Relatively singular

00:37:33.651 --> 00:37:38.522
It's not like when you build something like a social media app

00:37:38.522 --> 00:37:39.123
Or a game

00:37:39.123 --> 00:37:41.325
Where everyone might have different tastes

00:37:41.759 --> 00:37:43.194
And it might be hard

00:37:43.194 --> 00:37:46.330
To satisfy everyone's needs

00:37:46.831 --> 00:37:49.300
Then you might need recommendation algorithms

00:37:49.567 --> 00:37:50.701
But with coding

00:37:50.701 --> 00:37:54.138
The good thing is that excellent programmers writing code

00:37:54.138 --> 00:37:56.273
Actually have fairly similar styles

00:37:57.642 --> 00:37:58.342
What kind of style

00:37:58.342 --> 00:37:59.644
Clean and concise

00:37:59.644 --> 00:38:01.846
Yeah, right, good code is

00:38:01.846 --> 00:38:04.649
(Not messy) There are some shared standards

00:38:04.649 --> 00:38:05.383
For example, like you said

00:38:05.383 --> 00:38:06.984
The code is concise

00:38:07.284 --> 00:38:09.220
Structurally clear

00:38:09.220 --> 00:38:10.688
Suitable for future development

00:38:10.788 --> 00:38:12.523
And has reasonable abstractions

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:14.725
And of course many other standards

00:38:14.725 --> 00:38:18.496
But I think good programmers tend to have

00:38:18.496 --> 00:38:20.064
A fairly consensus-driven standard

00:38:20.064 --> 00:38:20.865
On this matter

00:38:20.931 --> 00:38:23.067
So from a product perspective

00:38:23.067 --> 00:38:26.270
It actually makes the coding product much simpler

00:38:27.304 --> 00:38:28.205
In your current work

00:38:28.205 --> 00:38:33.144
What percentage of code do you write with Claude Code

00:38:33.144 --> 00:38:37.048
How many times more productive does it make you

00:38:37.214 --> 00:38:39.216
You just asked a question that almost got me fired

00:38:39.216 --> 00:38:40.985
Google doesn't allow using Claude Code

00:38:40.985 --> 00:38:42.820
Hahahahaha, oh right

00:38:44.455 --> 00:38:47.758
I think, for me

00:38:49.627 --> 00:38:50.995
A conservative estimate

00:38:51.162 --> 00:38:54.598
Maybe 90% of the code is model-generated

00:38:54.598 --> 00:38:55.199
But it might be

00:38:55.199 --> 00:38:57.935
I need to spend a lot of time reviewing the code

00:38:57.935 --> 00:38:59.303
To see if it's written appropriately

00:38:59.303 --> 00:38:59.904
Written reasonably

00:38:59.904 --> 00:39:02.073
Whether it's really what I wanted it to write

00:39:02.206 --> 00:39:06.277
And I think after having AI-assisted tools

00:39:06.277 --> 00:39:08.579
The most important thing about writing code

00:39:08.579 --> 00:39:10.281
Has become

00:39:10.281 --> 00:39:11.882
How you design it

00:39:12.149 --> 00:39:14.685
How you design the logic of your code

00:39:14.685 --> 00:39:17.388
And which files it needs to interact with

00:39:17.388 --> 00:39:18.456
Files to associate with

00:39:18.456 --> 00:39:20.925
And what things need to be done

00:39:20.958 --> 00:39:22.193
And you need to give the model

00:39:22.193 --> 00:39:24.729
Maybe provide some reasonable context

00:39:24.862 --> 00:39:25.329
I mean, like

00:39:25.329 --> 00:39:25.996
For example, this code

00:39:25.996 --> 00:39:28.099
You can use it as a reference (参考) to take a look

00:39:28.099 --> 00:39:31.602
Right, actually outputting code

00:39:31.769 --> 00:39:34.739
I think models are way more capable than humans

00:39:35.005 --> 00:39:36.307
So for me

00:39:36.307 --> 00:39:37.875
If you actually count

00:39:37.875 --> 00:39:40.511
How many lines of code I wrote by hand

00:39:40.511 --> 00:39:41.779
How many lines of code the model wrote

00:39:41.779 --> 00:39:45.015
I'd say conservatively, the model wrote over 90%

00:39:45.082 --> 00:39:47.084
If not conservative, maybe 99% or 100%

00:39:48.119 --> 00:39:49.987
The remaining 10% is what it can't write

00:39:50.521 --> 00:39:51.956
Or why you didn't let it write

00:39:53.791 --> 00:39:55.059
Conservatively, 90%

00:39:55.059 --> 00:39:56.160
Giving myself some credit

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:57.695
Hahaha

00:39:57.695 --> 00:39:58.996
I think what it can't write

00:39:58.996 --> 00:40:01.332
And the part I can write is becoming less and less

00:40:01.332 --> 00:40:02.767
Less and less and less

00:40:02.933 --> 00:40:04.235
What was it like in the past

00:40:04.235 --> 00:40:05.536
It was what it couldn't write

00:40:06.804 --> 00:40:07.938
I think

00:40:07.938 --> 00:40:13.711
Very early on, maybe about a year and a half ago

00:40:14.412 --> 00:40:14.912
At that time

00:40:14.912 --> 00:40:15.780
To be honest, on the market

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:17.715
Only Claude was able to

00:40:17.715 --> 00:40:20.317
Actually write this kind of software engineering code

00:40:21.051 --> 00:40:21.786
At that time

00:40:21.786 --> 00:40:26.056
You could still feel many flaws in the model

00:40:26.056 --> 00:40:26.991
For example

00:40:26.991 --> 00:40:27.892
Sometimes when it wrote code

00:40:27.892 --> 00:40:29.693
It would only focus on this one file

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:32.429
It wouldn't pay much attention to multiple

00:40:32.429 --> 00:40:34.064
The relationships between multiple files

00:40:34.198 --> 00:40:37.868
And if, say, a class

00:40:37.868 --> 00:40:40.538
Its definition was buried many layers deep

00:40:40.538 --> 00:40:42.840
Or it wasn't directly nested in this

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:44.708
This direct tree structure

00:40:44.708 --> 00:40:46.544
The model probably couldn't find it

00:40:46.877 --> 00:40:49.814
Now I think this is happening less and less

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:50.514
Hehe

00:40:50.581 --> 00:40:51.515
Really less and less

00:40:51.515 --> 00:40:52.483
As a researcher

00:40:53.117 --> 00:40:55.753
Your programming workload

00:40:55.753 --> 00:40:57.688
How many times that of the past

00:40:57.688 --> 00:40:59.657
Because from the perspective of writing code

00:40:59.657 --> 00:41:00.724
It's quite hard to quantify this

00:41:00.724 --> 00:41:04.461
But if we talk about, say, running experiments

00:41:04.461 --> 00:41:07.765
And the efficiency of implementing ideas

00:41:07.765 --> 00:41:10.835
I think compared to a year or even a year and a half ago

00:41:10.835 --> 00:41:15.072
It could be 20 or even 50 times faster

00:41:15.472 --> 00:41:18.075
Right, because models have really become

00:41:18.509 --> 00:41:19.610
It can be pretty insane

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:21.278
You can open several at the same time

00:41:21.278 --> 00:41:22.546
And you have several ideas

00:41:22.546 --> 00:41:24.348
And test them simultaneously

00:41:24.348 --> 00:41:25.416
And sometimes even

00:41:25.416 --> 00:41:27.218
The model can help you monitor some experiments

00:41:27.218 --> 00:41:28.652
Monitor some results and stuff

00:41:28.886 --> 00:41:29.553
So

00:41:29.553 --> 00:41:32.356
It's really quite a significant efficiency boost

00:41:32.623 --> 00:41:35.259
Right, but

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:38.729
If we talk about personal working hours

00:41:38.729 --> 00:41:41.732
I feel like it has made my working hours longer

00:41:43.133 --> 00:41:43.968
Why is that

00:41:43.968 --> 00:41:44.768
It's just that

00:41:44.935 --> 00:41:46.871
Because development speed has increased

00:41:47.204 --> 00:41:48.105
The more you try, the more you want to try

00:41:48.239 --> 00:41:50.541
There are more and more ideas to try

00:41:50.608 --> 00:41:54.078
So it feels like before, you might have had this situation

00:41:54.078 --> 00:41:55.346
You have something

00:41:55.479 --> 00:41:56.080
Like this file

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:56.847
You haven't seen it before

00:41:57.348 --> 00:42:00.284
You might not quite understand it yourself

00:42:00.284 --> 00:42:02.386
Then you'd definitely have to spend time finding that person

00:42:02.486 --> 00:42:03.187
And you'd schedule

00:42:03.187 --> 00:42:04.889
That person, maybe a few hours later

00:42:05.189 --> 00:42:05.823
But now it's not like that

00:42:05.823 --> 00:42:06.824
You just see this file

00:42:06.824 --> 00:42:09.193
You don't understand it, just ask Claude or Gemini

00:42:09.460 --> 00:42:12.062
Gemini might tell you the result in five seconds

00:42:12.062 --> 00:42:13.364
And you just keep going

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:16.500
Hahaha, so in terms of working hours

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:18.202
I feel like working hours have actually gotten longer

00:42:18.202 --> 00:42:20.504
And the intensity has increased too

00:42:20.671 --> 00:42:22.206
Well, Google isn't that Google anymore

00:42:22.206 --> 00:42:22.907
Is that so

00:42:22.907 --> 00:42:23.641
Not that

00:42:23.641 --> 00:42:24.742
Google where you can coast along

00:42:26.277 --> 00:42:28.012
Not that work-life balance Google

00:42:28.479 --> 00:42:30.981
I feel like in the GenAI (生成式人工智能) field

00:42:31.081 --> 00:42:32.082
No one can just coast along

00:42:32.082 --> 00:42:32.816
Hahaha

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:34.151
So what hours are you keeping these days

00:42:34.652 --> 00:42:37.955
I usually start around 9 in the morning

00:42:38.422 --> 00:42:39.557
Get to the office at 9

00:42:41.458 --> 00:42:42.826
At 9 AM, I might first get up and check emails

00:42:42.826 --> 00:42:45.029
And look at the experiments from the night before

00:42:45.029 --> 00:42:47.331
Then get to the office around 10

00:42:47.331 --> 00:42:50.134
And then at night

00:42:50.134 --> 00:42:52.937
If I'm alone in the US

00:42:52.937 --> 00:42:55.172
I might stay until around 10 or 11

00:42:55.239 --> 00:42:57.875
Of course, if my family is here

00:42:57.875 --> 00:42:58.542
If my wife is here

00:42:58.542 --> 00:42:59.710
I might go home a bit earlier

00:42:59.710 --> 00:43:01.011
But at home I'd be working anyway

00:43:01.745 --> 00:43:04.915
So I think in the GenAI field

00:43:04.915 --> 00:43:06.583
No one is just lying around

00:43:06.583 --> 00:43:07.551
Unless

00:43:07.585 --> 00:43:10.387
You've completely lost interest in technology

00:43:10.387 --> 00:43:11.822
And have no ambition for yourself

00:43:12.022 --> 00:43:14.224
Then no one would care if you just lay there

00:43:14.825 --> 00:43:18.395
But I think most people are quite self-driven

00:43:18.395 --> 00:43:20.230
They just want to do it themselves, right

00:43:20.698 --> 00:43:21.932
Do you think other fields

00:43:21.932 --> 00:43:24.935
Will have more of these Claude Code moments

00:43:24.935 --> 00:43:26.503
Where will the next explosion happen after coding

00:43:27.938 --> 00:43:29.239
You asked a good question

00:43:29.807 --> 00:43:30.808
If I could see it clearly

00:43:30.808 --> 00:43:32.242
I might have gone out to start a company already

00:43:32.242 --> 00:43:35.479
Hahahaha

00:43:35.479 --> 00:43:36.647
Right, but but

00:43:36.647 --> 00:43:39.016
It's true that besides coding

00:43:39.083 --> 00:43:40.184
We can already see

00:43:40.184 --> 00:43:40.718
That many

00:43:40.718 --> 00:43:43.187
Other directions are already having a big impact

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:44.121
But if we only talk about those directions

00:43:44.121 --> 00:43:45.656
They might not be a good

00:43:45.856 --> 00:43:46.890
market direction

00:43:46.890 --> 00:43:48.292
Because

00:43:48.292 --> 00:43:51.695
Coding is special in that

00:43:51.695 --> 00:43:52.429
It itself is

00:43:52.429 --> 00:43:53.397
A very large market

00:43:53.464 --> 00:43:54.031
But if you look at some other directions

00:43:54.031 --> 00:43:56.467
They might not be

00:43:56.500 --> 00:44:00.637
Such a large market

00:44:00.637 --> 00:44:01.138
For example

00:44:01.138 --> 00:44:02.906
Some people say the next direction is

00:44:03.007 --> 00:44:05.643
This kind of

00:44:05.643 --> 00:44:07.644
AI-generated content or something

00:44:08.379 --> 00:44:11.615
But AI-generated content

00:44:11.615 --> 00:44:12.516
How big is that market

00:44:12.516 --> 00:44:14.218
Right

00:44:14.284 --> 00:44:15.653
I think

00:44:15.819 --> 00:44:17.287
If you say this content

00:44:17.388 --> 00:44:18.856
Is for people to consume

00:44:18.856 --> 00:44:20.124
Then people have limited time

00:44:20.124 --> 00:44:24.461
No matter how much content you generate

00:44:25.295 --> 00:44:27.398
People's time is only 24 hours a day

00:44:27.398 --> 00:44:29.199
Right

00:44:29.199 --> 00:44:30.167
Unless it completely replaces people

00:44:30.167 --> 00:44:31.135
Like replacing TV

00:44:31.135 --> 00:44:32.936
Then that would be another story

00:44:32.936 --> 00:44:35.606
Like the Vision Pro that came out before

00:44:35.606 --> 00:44:36.173
Then that would be another story

00:44:36.173 --> 00:44:37.574
But that would be

00:44:38.275 --> 00:44:39.743
A bigger story

00:44:39.743 --> 00:44:40.144
So I think

00:44:40.144 --> 00:44:42.780
Besides coding

00:44:43.047 --> 00:44:44.982
Everyone is still looking for

00:44:44.982 --> 00:44:45.916
The next big market

00:44:45.916 --> 00:44:49.253
And if there is one

00:44:49.653 --> 00:44:51.021
I think there will be

00:44:51.021 --> 00:44:52.956
But it's just

00:44:52.956 --> 00:44:53.724
Not necessarily that big

00:44:54.224 --> 00:44:55.626
I think the most likely one might be

00:44:55.626 --> 00:44:56.593
This kind of interactive education

00:44:56.593 --> 00:44:57.995
Or maybe

00:44:57.995 --> 00:45:01.098
You said coding is not a direction for you

00:45:01.365 --> 00:45:03.534
Because coding itself is already very big

00:45:04.501 --> 00:45:05.669
Yeah, it's already a huge market

00:45:05.669 --> 00:45:07.805
Do you think AI researchers

00:45:07.805 --> 00:45:10.507
How should we treat coding

00:45:11.208 --> 00:45:12.810
Should we use coding to validate our ideas

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:16.246
Or should we make coding itself the end goal

00:45:16.613 --> 00:45:19.750
I think there are two types of people

00:45:19.950 --> 00:45:21.719
One type is

00:45:21.719 --> 00:45:22.453
They genuinely want to make coding better

00:45:22.953 --> 00:45:24.621
Another type is

00:45:25.255 --> 00:45:26.924
They want to use coding

00:45:26.924 --> 00:45:27.858
As a means to validate AI capabilities

00:45:27.858 --> 00:45:28.325
I think both are fine

00:45:28.325 --> 00:45:29.193
Both directions are fine

00:45:29.460 --> 00:45:30.794
But I think

00:45:30.861 --> 00:45:31.929
The people who genuinely want to make coding better

00:45:32.196 --> 00:45:33.731
They need to think more about products

00:45:33.731 --> 00:45:34.264
And the people who want to validate AI capabilities

00:45:34.531 --> 00:45:35.766
They need to think more about

00:45:35.766 --> 00:45:37.367
How to build better benchmarks

00:45:37.768 --> 00:45:40.838
Right

00:45:40.838 --> 00:45:42.172
I think both directions are very meaningful

00:45:42.439 --> 00:45:43.774
Just their focus is different

00:45:45.476 --> 00:45:48.645
Do you think

00:45:48.645 --> 00:45:49.646
The current state of AI research

00:45:50.481 --> 00:45:50.881
Is more like

00:45:50.881 --> 00:45:51.982
A gold rush

00:45:51.982 --> 00:45:52.616
Or more like

00:45:52.616 --> 00:45:53.417
A scientific revolution

00:45:54.618 --> 00:45:57.054
I think it's a bit of both

00:45:57.054 --> 00:45:58.455
Like those things AI

00:45:58.856 --> 00:46:01.458
There are many things that AI actually can't easily do

00:46:01.458 --> 00:46:05.829
But conversely, humans might do better

00:46:05.829 --> 00:46:07.731
For example, being a product manager

00:46:09.399 --> 00:46:11.368
To be honest, I think

00:46:11.368 --> 00:46:12.703
Being a good product manager

00:46:12.703 --> 00:46:14.771
Is something I currently can't figure out

00:46:14.771 --> 00:46:16.874
How to train AI to do

00:46:17.841 --> 00:46:18.642
Why is that

00:46:19.042 --> 00:46:19.977
There's no standard

00:46:19.977 --> 00:46:21.111
There's no standard (no metric)

00:46:21.111 --> 00:46:22.913
Like what makes a good product

00:46:23.847 --> 00:46:25.649
I can't really figure it out

00:46:25.649 --> 00:46:26.683
There's no very objective standard

00:46:26.683 --> 00:46:29.520
You have to build it and let people use it

00:46:29.520 --> 00:46:30.521
Only then do you know it's good

00:46:30.521 --> 00:46:31.889
Then everyone will say it's good

00:46:32.256 --> 00:46:33.357
Right, I think

00:46:33.690 --> 00:46:36.193
That's something with very unclear feedback signals

00:46:36.326 --> 00:46:38.495
Then I don't know how to train AI to do that

00:46:38.962 --> 00:46:39.462
Right

00:46:40.430 --> 00:46:43.000
When will programmers be completely replaced

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:44.334
Will there be such a day

00:46:45.569 --> 00:46:46.336
Mm-hmm

00:46:48.105 --> 00:46:52.276
I think that day will come

00:46:52.476 --> 00:46:55.112
But it won't come all at once

00:46:55.679 --> 00:46:58.382
It won't be like programmers are all still there

00:46:58.482 --> 00:46:59.383
And after one night

00:46:59.383 --> 00:47:01.485
The next day all programmers are fired

00:47:01.485 --> 00:47:02.319
It won't be like that

00:47:02.319 --> 00:47:03.887
It will definitely be a gradual process

00:47:04.454 --> 00:47:06.523
But
Everyone can already see this gradual process now

00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:09.126
Because some companies have already started laying people off

00:47:09.526 --> 00:47:11.461
Right, I think

00:47:11.762 --> 00:47:13.330
In a sense

00:47:14.064 --> 00:47:15.265
AI is a

00:47:16.133 --> 00:47:16.700
In a sense

00:47:16.700 --> 00:47:17.768
Of course it's a very good thing

00:47:17.768 --> 00:47:18.569
But from another perspective

00:47:18.569 --> 00:47:19.336
It might also be

00:47:19.336 --> 00:47:21.004
A very unfortunate thing

00:47:21.004 --> 00:47:24.007
That AI is a very centralized technology

00:47:24.007 --> 00:47:27.077
It will make a small number of people stronger

00:47:27.511 --> 00:47:30.280
But will make most people lose

00:47:30.514 --> 00:47:32.382
Their unique value

00:47:32.916 --> 00:47:33.851
Right, so I think

00:47:33.851 --> 00:47:37.254
For traditional software engineering

00:47:39.323 --> 00:47:41.124
The final result might be

00:47:41.124 --> 00:47:45.462
Now 1/1000 of the people do the work of everyone in the past

00:47:45.896 --> 00:47:47.965
Earning 100 times the current salary

00:47:49.266 --> 00:47:51.335
Then what advice do you have for programmers

00:47:54.204 --> 00:47:55.339
I think

00:47:55.339 --> 00:47:58.375
Haha, I think maybe

00:47:59.776 --> 00:48:00.878
Embrace new things

00:48:00.878 --> 00:48:02.679
I think that's very important

00:48:02.913 --> 00:48:03.480
I think

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:05.482
One very important thing for future programmers might be

00:48:05.482 --> 00:48:07.484
How to effectively collaborate with AI

00:48:07.885 --> 00:48:08.685
Mm-hmm, like

00:48:08.685 --> 00:48:11.188
There are many things that AI might do

00:48:11.188 --> 00:48:12.022
Not that well

00:48:12.022 --> 00:48:13.190
Like how to

00:48:13.624 --> 00:48:17.060
Reasonably design an implementation plan for something

00:48:17.427 --> 00:48:19.029
And how to design it

00:48:19.029 --> 00:48:20.864
So that it might align with the company's

00:48:20.864 --> 00:48:22.599
Future development

00:48:22.599 --> 00:48:23.233
Those kinds of things

00:48:23.233 --> 00:48:25.269
You might have a hard time telling a model

00:48:25.269 --> 00:48:26.436
To make it understand these things

00:48:26.703 --> 00:48:28.839
Those things might still need humans to do

00:48:29.406 --> 00:48:30.507
But maybe things like specific

00:48:30.507 --> 00:48:31.408
Very specific

00:48:31.408 --> 00:48:33.243
Like the work many programmers did in the past

00:48:33.243 --> 00:48:37.781
Where your manager tells you to implement this plan

00:48:37.981 --> 00:48:39.116
And give it to me by next Friday

00:48:39.650 --> 00:48:40.284
I think that kind of work

00:48:40.284 --> 00:48:42.486
Might not exist in the future

00:48:42.886 --> 00:48:45.088
Then what kind of programmers would be in that 1/1000

00:48:45.088 --> 00:48:46.089
What are their traits

00:48:46.156 --> 00:48:47.357
1/1000 is just a figurative number

00:48:47.357 --> 00:48:48.759
I really don't know if it would be 1/1000

00:48:48.759 --> 00:48:49.493
Or 1/10,000

00:48:49.493 --> 00:48:50.694
Or 1/100,000

00:48:50.827 --> 00:48:51.795
Or maybe 1%

00:48:51.795 --> 00:48:52.596
Don't be so pessimistic

00:48:54.197 --> 00:48:55.766
I'm a famous pessimist

00:48:55.766 --> 00:48:57.834
So don't take it too seriously

00:48:58.068 --> 00:48:59.136
And

00:49:00.404 --> 00:49:01.672
I think

00:49:01.672 --> 00:49:02.940
Good programmers in the future

00:49:02.940 --> 00:49:05.842
First, technically speaking

00:49:05.842 --> 00:49:07.244
They will definitely be very strong

00:49:07.344 --> 00:49:08.845
Because if you're technically weak

00:49:09.012 --> 00:49:10.013
There's no reason

00:49:10.013 --> 00:49:10.947
Why AI can't replace you

00:49:11.515 --> 00:49:13.850
But being technically strong might not be the only thing

00:49:13.850 --> 00:49:14.952
It won't be a necessary condition

00:49:14.952 --> 00:49:16.053
It might be a sufficient condition

00:49:16.353 --> 00:49:18.121
Another thing I think will be very important

00:49:18.121 --> 00:49:22.592
is that you have to understand how your part of the work

00:49:22.592 --> 00:49:25.429
fits into a large organization or a big company

00:49:25.929 --> 00:49:27.998
how to

00:49:27.998 --> 00:49:29.800
how to adapt and integrate into it (Mm-hmm)

00:49:29.800 --> 00:49:31.268
This might also be an important thing

00:49:31.435 --> 00:49:31.835
Mm-hmm, and

00:49:31.835 --> 00:49:34.972
And of course there might be many other things

00:49:34.972 --> 00:49:35.772
For example

00:49:35.872 --> 00:49:38.208
whether this person's planning ability is strong enough

00:49:38.308 --> 00:49:39.276
If their planning ability is strong

00:49:39.276 --> 00:49:41.778
they can definitely take this big

00:49:41.778 --> 00:49:42.913
very complex thing

00:49:42.913 --> 00:49:44.781
and break it down into many relatively smaller things

00:49:44.781 --> 00:49:46.350
and hand them over to different AIs to do

00:49:46.717 --> 00:49:51.288
But right now these three abilities seem important

00:49:51.588 --> 00:49:53.190
Things that AI might not be able to fully do yet

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:54.591
doesn't mean it won't be able to in six months

00:49:54.958 --> 00:49:56.526
Maybe in six months you come ask me

00:49:56.526 --> 00:49:58.495
I find that the last thing AI can already do

00:49:58.729 --> 00:49:59.596
Then only two things remain

00:49:59.596 --> 00:50:00.163
Another six months later

00:50:00.163 --> 00:50:01.665
Maybe the remaining two can also be done

00:50:01.765 --> 00:50:04.067
Then maybe my answer would become more pessimistic

00:50:04.134 --> 00:50:04.668
So

00:50:04.668 --> 00:50:06.470
No one can predict what will happen in six months

00:50:06.470 --> 00:50:08.138
I can only speak from the current perspective

00:50:10.407 --> 00:50:11.508
That past Spring Festival

00:50:11.508 --> 00:50:13.877
Another thing many people paid attention to was Seedance

00:50:13.877 --> 00:50:15.379
Will Seedance make Google anxious

00:50:15.379 --> 00:50:17.514
I think actually

00:50:19.549 --> 00:50:20.450
Possibly yes

00:50:20.751 --> 00:50:22.586
But this anxiety

00:50:22.586 --> 00:50:24.187
Hasn't reached me yet

00:50:24.488 --> 00:50:27.791
Maybe it gives the Google DeepMind

00:50:27.791 --> 00:50:29.993
team responsible for multimodal generation

00:50:29.993 --> 00:50:31.228
some pressure

00:50:31.695 --> 00:50:35.732
But if you ask me

00:50:35.732 --> 00:50:36.066
I think

00:50:36.066 --> 00:50:38.635
I might not think they have much to be anxious about

00:50:39.136 --> 00:50:40.303
Like I think

00:50:40.303 --> 00:50:42.706
It doesn't reflect any paradigm shift

00:50:43.173 --> 00:50:45.275
More importantly, I think ByteDance

00:50:45.409 --> 00:50:48.879
whether it's the product effect

00:50:48.879 --> 00:50:51.481
or possibly in terms of data and such

00:50:51.648 --> 00:50:53.717
These details are done very very well

00:50:53.884 --> 00:50:56.253
I think indeed

00:50:56.520 --> 00:50:57.888
ByteDance has historically had

00:50:57.888 --> 00:51:00.457
a relatively strong advantage in multimodal generation

00:51:01.124 --> 00:51:02.893
But I think at least personally

00:51:02.893 --> 00:51:03.527
I haven't experienced

00:51:03.527 --> 00:51:05.429
that it's a paradigm shift

00:51:06.163 --> 00:51:07.798
Then maybe

00:51:08.365 --> 00:51:11.468
It's not enough to make everyone very anxious

00:51:11.968 --> 00:51:13.770
Right but there is definitely pressure

00:51:14.171 --> 00:51:14.538
Right

00:51:14.538 --> 00:51:17.407
Does Seedance's product capability come from model capability

00:51:17.407 --> 00:51:18.408
Or product capability

00:51:18.809 --> 00:51:21.378
I haven't worked

00:51:21.411 --> 00:51:22.479
At ByteDance

00:51:22.479 --> 00:51:23.246
So I don't

00:51:23.246 --> 00:51:24.481
know the specific details

00:51:24.514 --> 00:51:25.615
But if you ask me to guess

00:51:25.615 --> 00:51:27.517
I think the model probably accounts for the majority

00:51:27.884 --> 00:51:28.485
Mm-hmm

00:51:29.252 --> 00:51:31.421
What does good model capability come from

00:51:31.421 --> 00:51:32.122
Comes from data

00:51:32.122 --> 00:51:34.224
Because there probably isn't fundamental innovation in algorithms

00:51:34.724 --> 00:51:36.026
I think algorithms

00:51:36.026 --> 00:51:37.527
First of all because multimodal belongs to

00:51:37.527 --> 00:51:38.361
what we just said, still belongs to that

00:51:38.361 --> 00:51:39.162
scientific problem

00:51:39.162 --> 00:51:41.364
Multimodal generation belongs to scientific problems

00:51:41.364 --> 00:51:42.032
Right, multimodal generation

00:51:42.032 --> 00:51:44.501
Still belongs to a relatively scientific problem (Has multimodal understanding been solved)

00:51:45.635 --> 00:51:48.805
Compared to generation it's definitely more systematic

00:51:48.805 --> 00:51:50.140
Has a more systematic understanding

00:51:50.140 --> 00:51:54.978
But compared to text tokens

00:51:54.978 --> 00:51:58.115
Definitely still not that

00:51:58.115 --> 00:51:59.082
The paradigm isn't that fixed yet

00:51:59.483 --> 00:52:00.917
I think in generation it might be

00:52:01.218 --> 00:52:02.986
Because it's still something

00:52:03.053 --> 00:52:04.020
where the paradigm hasn't been fixed

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:07.757
Maybe each company uses somewhat different techniques

00:52:07.757 --> 00:52:08.959
big or small differences

00:52:09.326 --> 00:52:13.363
And um right now we can mostly just see

00:52:13.363 --> 00:52:14.397
In terms of effects

00:52:14.397 --> 00:52:17.267
Maybe ByteDance and Google DeepMind are

00:52:17.267 --> 00:52:17.801
In terms of effects

00:52:17.801 --> 00:52:18.935
The two that do it better

00:52:19.102 --> 00:52:21.771
Mm-hmm, so it might also come from details

00:52:21.771 --> 00:52:22.839
Done better

00:52:22.973 --> 00:52:24.875
Right if you ask me to guess

00:52:24.875 --> 00:52:25.942
I would guess data

