00:53:30.273 --> 00:53:33.944
You say, taking a snapshot in Q1 2026

00:53:33.944 --> 00:53:36.479
Do you think the capability gap between Chinese and US models

00:53:36.479 --> 00:53:38.648
is widening or narrowing?

00:53:38.648 --> 00:53:39.549
How far apart are they?

00:53:40.083 --> 00:53:40.784
I think

00:53:40.784 --> 00:53:41.318
Um

00:53:42.819 --> 00:53:44.287
If we take a snapshot right now

00:53:44.287 --> 00:53:49.059
and look at the development trends over the past year

00:53:49.059 --> 00:53:50.827
or the past year and a half

00:53:51.094 --> 00:53:51.661
Obviously

00:53:51.661 --> 00:53:55.098
the gap between China and the US is getting smaller and smaller

00:53:55.966 --> 00:53:58.735
But whether this gap will eventually close completely

00:53:58.735 --> 00:54:00.103
or even if China surpasses the US

00:54:00.503 --> 00:54:04.741
I think that's an open question

00:54:04.841 --> 00:54:08.878
I think for Chinese AI researchers

00:54:08.878 --> 00:54:10.614
and research institutions, it's also an opportunity

00:54:11.348 --> 00:54:13.149
And

00:54:14.084 --> 00:54:16.019
I think one very real thing is

00:54:16.019 --> 00:54:16.820
that

00:54:17.153 --> 00:54:20.557
China is indeed at a significant disadvantage in terms of actual compute resources

00:54:20.557 --> 00:54:22.425
It's at a big disadvantage

00:54:23.426 --> 00:54:25.528
But this significant disadvantage

00:54:25.662 --> 00:54:28.064
may have actually forced out some interesting things

00:54:28.431 --> 00:54:29.933
For example, Chinese model companies

00:54:29.933 --> 00:54:33.470
are actually quite good at distilling from others

00:54:34.070 --> 00:54:34.570
Right

00:54:34.638 --> 00:54:37.407
Recently Dario (Anthropic Co-founder and CEO) called out three companies for distilling from them

00:54:39.943 --> 00:54:42.746
I think distillation itself

00:54:42.746 --> 00:54:46.683
is actually an open secret

00:54:47.183 --> 00:54:50.420
But I think there are different ways to approach distillation

00:54:51.154 --> 00:54:55.025
There's brute-force distillation and smart distillation

00:54:55.025 --> 00:54:57.027
two different approaches

00:54:58.161 --> 00:54:58.962
Um

00:54:59.829 --> 00:55:00.864
What do you mean by brute-force distillation?

00:55:01.698 --> 00:55:04.668
To give the simplest example of brute-force distillation:

00:55:04.668 --> 00:55:05.468
It's

00:55:05.468 --> 00:55:10.473
taking a bunch of tokens generated by Claude

00:55:10.740 --> 00:55:14.044
and forcibly training on them

00:55:15.345 --> 00:55:16.913
If you do something like this

00:55:16.913 --> 00:55:18.148
I feel

00:55:19.549 --> 00:55:21.318
First, it's not very ethical from a business standpoint

00:55:22.052 --> 00:55:25.322
And intellectually, it's rather foolish

00:55:26.589 --> 00:55:29.259
Because the companies doing this

00:55:29.259 --> 00:55:30.860
essentially

00:55:30.860 --> 00:55:32.262
demonstrate one thing

00:55:32.262 --> 00:55:34.297
they don't even know what they want to do

00:55:35.432 --> 00:55:37.434
The only thing they can do is copy others

00:55:37.500 --> 00:55:38.702
and make their model

00:55:38.702 --> 00:55:40.003
look a bit better on the benchmarks

00:55:40.370 --> 00:55:41.504
Right, but essentially it shows that

00:55:41.504 --> 00:55:42.772
they don't even know what they should be doing

00:55:43.573 --> 00:55:44.374
That's brute-force distillation

00:55:44.674 --> 00:55:45.475
But

00:55:45.508 --> 00:55:49.245
actually, distillation also involves some very interesting scientific questions

00:55:49.846 --> 00:55:53.483
For example, is there a possibility that

00:55:53.483 --> 00:55:54.184
Just a random example

00:55:54.184 --> 00:55:55.218
Like, could it be that

00:55:55.218 --> 00:55:56.052
in my process of generating

00:55:56.052 --> 00:55:58.121
my own training data pipeline

00:55:58.121 --> 00:56:00.090
I use other models as assistants

00:56:01.124 --> 00:56:04.294
Or the answers generated by my own model

00:56:04.294 --> 00:56:06.863
use other models as their evaluators

00:56:08.231 --> 00:56:11.868
This is actually, I think, commercially

00:56:11.868 --> 00:56:13.069
a bit of a gray area

00:56:13.403 --> 00:56:15.805
But from a technical perspective, it's quite interesting

00:56:16.373 --> 00:56:19.809
Because if you think about it, in a sense

00:56:20.310 --> 00:56:22.178
Chinese labs may have become

00:56:22.178 --> 00:56:24.781
pioneers in Multi-Agent (multi-agent) training

00:56:25.782 --> 00:56:26.583
Oh

00:56:26.616 --> 00:56:28.618
And it's true Multi-Agent

00:56:29.052 --> 00:56:31.755
Because if they use models from different companies

00:56:32.122 --> 00:56:33.757
with these smarter approaches

00:56:33.757 --> 00:56:36.059
and integrate them into a single training system

00:56:36.226 --> 00:56:39.963
each model's distribution might be very different

00:56:39.963 --> 00:56:42.432
The distribution of their language is very different

00:56:42.832 --> 00:56:45.935
This is true Multi-Agent

00:56:45.935 --> 00:56:47.537
It might be more so than

00:56:47.737 --> 00:56:50.340
for example, using several Geminis together

00:56:50.407 --> 00:56:52.108
It's something more technically interesting

00:56:52.709 --> 00:56:57.514
So I think, for me, the distillation of intelligence

00:56:57.547 --> 00:56:58.515
I don't know, commercially

00:56:58.515 --> 00:57:01.017
whether it'll end up being clearly wrong

00:57:01.017 --> 00:57:02.218
or clearly right

00:57:02.418 --> 00:57:03.720
But technically it's actually quite interesting

00:57:05.021 --> 00:57:06.823
Which companies are you referring to with these two types of distillation?

00:57:08.858 --> 00:57:10.226
Can we bleep out the names in post-production?

00:57:10.226 --> 00:57:11.394
(Sure) Hahahaha

00:57:12.495 --> 00:57:15.064
First of all, I haven't worked in a Chinese lab（实验室）

00:57:15.064 --> 00:57:16.533
So I don't know exactly who

00:57:17.267 --> 00:57:19.202
But my feeling is

00:57:19.502 --> 00:57:19.903
XXX

00:57:19.903 --> 00:57:22.038
probably used hard distillation

00:57:22.038 --> 00:57:24.741
And XXX might have done hard distillation before

00:57:25.208 --> 00:57:27.644
But later they probably gradually tried

00:57:27.644 --> 00:57:29.612
to shift toward soft distillation

00:57:29.946 --> 00:57:31.514
I think it's fairly obvious

00:57:31.514 --> 00:57:33.883
The one that probably distills less is ByteDance

00:57:34.818 --> 00:57:36.953
I feel like ByteDance's model

00:57:36.953 --> 00:57:38.254
is still quite distinctive

00:57:39.489 --> 00:57:41.157
Hmm, what makes it distinctive?

00:57:41.191 --> 00:57:42.492
For example, this model

00:57:42.659 --> 00:57:44.594
How smart would you say it is?

00:57:44.594 --> 00:57:45.461
I think

00:57:45.495 --> 00:57:50.567
Doubao is definitely not as smart as Gemini or Claude

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:52.602
But first of all, Doubao

00:57:52.602 --> 00:57:55.071
For example, Doubao's voice generation is extremely good

00:57:55.138 --> 00:57:56.005
Wait, is that difficult?

00:57:56.005 --> 00:57:59.476
Technically, Doubao is indeed the best at it

00:57:59.476 --> 00:58:00.910
Because I find that for life questions

00:58:00.910 --> 00:58:01.678
I just want to ask Doubao

00:58:01.678 --> 00:58:02.545
Because it's so fast

00:58:02.545 --> 00:58:03.246
But other models

00:58:03.246 --> 00:58:05.248
Why don't they optimize this product feature?

00:58:05.548 --> 00:58:08.585
I think it still has to do with their user base

00:58:09.419 --> 00:58:09.953
In the US

00:58:09.953 --> 00:58:15.492
I think people are more focused on

00:58:15.492 --> 00:58:17.794
