01:13:50.659 --> 01:13:51.293
My high school

01:13:51.293 --> 01:13:52.261
Anyway I think

01:13:52.261 --> 01:13:54.730
I wasn't at the level of making the national training team

01:13:54.730 --> 01:13:55.597
So let's not talk about that

01:13:56.031 --> 01:13:59.134
But before taking the senior year competition exam

01:13:59.668 --> 01:14:02.938
By a twist of fate I went to Tsinghua for a summer camp

01:14:03.472 --> 01:14:06.208
And by a twist of fate on the last day of the summer camp

01:14:06.208 --> 01:14:08.410
I heard they were doing

01:14:08.911 --> 01:14:10.145
Independent enrollment

01:14:10.512 --> 01:14:12.514
But mainly aimed at Beijing students

01:14:13.282 --> 01:14:16.718
I frantically texted the admissions office teacher

01:14:16.718 --> 01:14:18.053
Saying I wanted to take the exam with them

01:14:19.321 --> 01:14:20.489
He agreed

01:14:20.489 --> 01:14:22.724
And then he agreed to let us take the exam

01:14:22.858 --> 01:14:24.293
You all or just you

01:14:24.293 --> 01:14:24.827
Just agreed

01:14:24.827 --> 01:14:27.830
Me
And the few people from our high school who went together

01:14:27.863 --> 01:14:31.433
Those high school classmates from Shanghai who went to that summer camp

01:14:31.834 --> 01:14:33.902
Oh what reason did you use to convince him to text him

01:14:34.203 --> 01:14:35.771
I've forgotten the specifics of that text

01:14:35.771 --> 01:14:37.706
But the general idea of that text was

01:14:37.840 --> 01:14:39.107
You give Beijing students the exam

01:14:39.107 --> 01:14:40.242
Why not give Shanghai students the exam

01:14:41.210 --> 01:14:42.678
Oh, you were quite righteous about it

01:14:42.811 --> 01:14:46.281
Did you think they were playing favorites at that time

01:14:46.515 --> 01:14:47.516
I didn't think they were playing favorites

01:14:47.516 --> 01:14:48.951
I just felt they had this opportunity

01:14:48.951 --> 01:14:49.952
Why not give it to us

01:14:50.052 --> 01:14:52.187
Everyone's competing on the same playing field

01:14:52.321 --> 01:14:53.422
You were classmates at that time

01:14:54.122 --> 01:14:56.258
And so I sent this message

01:14:56.258 --> 01:14:58.293
And they actually let us take the exam

01:14:58.293 --> 01:14:58.793
How many people

01:14:59.228 --> 01:15:01.129
I can't quite remember

01:15:01.129 --> 01:15:03.031
Maybe from Shanghai

01:15:03.332 --> 01:15:05.934
There were probably about seven or eight people in that exam room

01:15:06.001 --> 01:15:06.935
You sent that text

01:15:07.469 --> 01:15:07.970
Maybe

01:15:07.970 --> 01:15:10.272
Maybe other high schools had other students who sent texts too

01:15:10.272 --> 01:15:11.540
But from our high school I was the one who sent it

01:15:12.975 --> 01:15:14.109
Oh so

01:15:14.109 --> 01:15:15.210
They were all Shanghai high schools

01:15:15.210 --> 01:15:16.178
Students who went to Beijing for that summer camp

01:15:16.178 --> 01:15:17.045
Students who attended the summer camp

01:15:17.179 --> 01:15:18.046
Students who attended the summer camp

01:15:19.047 --> 01:15:21.283
And then they let us take the exam

01:15:21.717 --> 01:15:23.452
And then we signed

01:15:23.452 --> 01:15:24.453
That easy to talk to

01:15:25.654 --> 01:15:28.724
Right, so what I learned from that incident

01:15:28.790 --> 01:15:30.792
The most important life lesson is

01:15:31.193 --> 01:15:32.094
Be bold

01:15:32.127 --> 01:15:32.627
Haha

01:15:34.062 --> 01:15:36.231
If you don't fight for it you'll never get it

01:15:36.231 --> 01:15:37.833
Even if you fight for it you might not get it

01:15:37.866 --> 01:15:39.434
But if you don't fight for it you definitely won't get it

01:15:39.935 --> 01:15:41.537
Were you nervous when you sent that text

01:15:41.537 --> 01:15:42.971
You were still in high school

01:15:44.606 --> 01:15:46.141
I can't remember anymore

01:15:46.341 --> 01:15:46.975
At that time I felt

01:15:46.975 --> 01:15:48.644
Was this a very bold thing for me

01:15:49.378 --> 01:15:51.947
No, at that time I was completely thinking

01:15:52.314 --> 01:15:53.215
I have to fight for it now

01:15:53.215 --> 01:15:55.450
If I don't fight for it today I won't be able to fight for it tomorrow haha

01:15:56.551 --> 01:15:57.119
Like

01:15:57.119 --> 01:16:00.222
The day I heard about it I immediately started frantically texting

01:16:00.589 --> 01:16:01.523
Frantically texting who

01:16:01.556 --> 01:16:02.424
Texting the admissions office

01:16:02.424 --> 01:16:03.458
That Tsinghua admissions office teacher

01:16:03.592 --> 01:16:05.460
Texting one person or multiple people

01:16:05.961 --> 01:16:07.262
Can't remember, probably one teacher

01:16:07.362 --> 01:16:09.197
Did he reply quickly

01:16:09.197 --> 01:16:11.667
Mm-hmm mm-hmm I think Tsinghua

01:16:11.667 --> 01:16:12.634
Just said yes

01:16:12.634 --> 01:16:14.870
I don't know if they discussed it among themselves

01:16:14.870 --> 01:16:17.472
But anyway in the end they said they agreed

01:16:18.373 --> 01:16:19.141
And then we took the exam together

01:16:19.174 --> 01:16:19.942
Right

01:16:20.008 --> 01:16:21.009
So I so I

01:16:21.009 --> 01:16:22.010
Why do I feel like

01:16:22.010 --> 01:16:23.645
I've always had quite a soft spot for Tsinghua

01:16:23.645 --> 01:16:23.946
I just feel

01:16:23.946 --> 01:16:28.817
that this school is willing to give people opportunities

01:16:28.951 --> 01:16:30.118
to provide equal opportunities for everyone

01:16:31.420 --> 01:16:32.487
How did you do on that exam?

01:16:33.789 --> 01:16:37.492
Well, when I came out, I felt like I totally bombed it

01:16:37.492 --> 01:16:39.094
Because I couldn't solve half a problem

01:16:39.695 --> 01:16:41.496
But later I found out others missed even more

01:16:42.497 --> 01:16:43.298
So I did get in after all

01:16:43.298 --> 01:16:46.568
Hahaha, yeah, exactly

01:16:46.735 --> 01:16:49.571
How many of your Shanghai classmates got in that year?

01:16:49.972 --> 01:16:50.939
Ah, I think two

01:16:51.907 --> 01:16:52.341
Independent recruitment

01:16:52.341 --> 01:16:53.308
Was it a score reduction or something?

01:16:53.342 --> 01:16:54.810
It lowered the cutoff to the first-tier university line

01:16:54.977 --> 01:16:55.577
Lowered to the first-tier line

01:16:55.577 --> 01:16:57.045
Oh

01:16:57.179 --> 01:16:58.513
So how did you do on the gaokao?

01:16:59.114 --> 01:17:02.150
Later, sure enough, my gaokao wasn't high enough for Tsinghua

01:17:02.150 --> 01:17:05.420
But I could get into any school except Tsinghua and Peking University

01:17:06.421 --> 01:17:06.989
Oh

01:17:08.056 --> 01:17:08.924
So why

01:17:09.791 --> 01:17:11.360
Online it says you were recommended for admission

01:17:12.294 --> 01:17:14.863
I think it's just that people

01:17:14.863 --> 01:17:17.165
who didn't go to school during those years find it hard

01:17:17.165 --> 01:17:18.734
hard to really understand what happened back then

01:17:18.734 --> 01:17:21.703
Because two cohorts before mine

01:17:21.703 --> 01:17:23.839
you could still get recommended admission with a provincial first prize

01:17:24.506 --> 01:17:27.075
A provincial first prize got you recommended admission

01:17:27.309 --> 01:17:28.210
What about your time?

01:17:28.310 --> 01:17:30.512
In our time, with a provincial first prize

01:17:30.512 --> 01:17:31.480
you made the provincial team

01:17:31.480 --> 01:17:34.049
then represented the provincial team at the national competition

01:17:34.049 --> 01:17:36.351
and only by making the national training team could you get recommended admission

01:17:36.718 --> 01:17:38.587
I made the provincial team and went to the national competition

01:17:38.587 --> 01:17:39.988
But I didn't make the national training team

01:17:40.355 --> 01:17:42.758
Right
So in my year, I didn't have a recommended admission slot

01:17:43.058 --> 01:17:43.859
Oh

01:17:44.993 --> 01:17:47.396
Were you good at competitions?

01:17:47.396 --> 01:17:49.131
I think I was pretty mediocre

01:17:49.664 --> 01:17:50.332
Like

01:17:50.332 --> 01:17:53.201
Isn't not being the best basically the same as being mediocre?

01:17:53.268 --> 01:17:54.703
And I obviously wasn't the best

01:17:54.703 --> 01:17:55.804
So I was just mediocre

01:17:58.473 --> 01:18:00.976
What was your family's attitude toward you doing competitions?

01:18:00.976 --> 01:18:02.144
What was their attitude?

01:18:03.412 --> 01:18:06.181
The best thing about my parents is

01:18:06.181 --> 01:18:07.349
they didn't really interfere much

01:18:07.549 --> 01:18:09.084
They may have tried to control me at some point

01:18:09.084 --> 01:18:10.185
but later found they couldn't

01:18:10.652 --> 01:18:11.453
Oh, how so?

01:18:11.453 --> 01:18:12.788
I just didn't listen to them

01:18:12.788 --> 01:18:13.555
Oh

01:18:14.589 --> 01:18:19.895
I think most Chinese families

01:18:20.095 --> 01:18:22.597
it's already considered pretty good when kids discuss things with their parents

01:18:23.398 --> 01:18:24.633
I usually just informed them

01:18:25.500 --> 01:18:26.601
Haha, informed them of what?

01:18:27.169 --> 01:18:29.838
Informed them, oh, I'm going to the independent recruitment exam

01:18:30.205 --> 01:18:31.940
Yeah, and

01:18:32.641 --> 01:18:35.243
Including filling out applications for high school and college

01:18:35.243 --> 01:18:38.180
My parents might not have even seen my application forms

01:18:38.580 --> 01:18:41.049
Oh, they're pretty laid-back, huh?

01:18:41.850 --> 01:18:45.287
I think they just

01:18:47.456 --> 01:18:49.324
when you can't understand what someone is doing

01:18:49.324 --> 01:18:51.526
the best thing is to not meddle

01:18:51.526 --> 01:18:53.361
I think my parents understood this very well

01:18:53.628 --> 01:18:56.898
Yeah, hahaha

01:18:57.132 --> 01:18:59.101
So you're pretty rebellious, huh?

01:19:00.635 --> 01:19:02.003
I think I am

01:19:03.872 --> 01:19:05.640
Pretty

01:19:05.640 --> 01:19:07.809
My personality is

01:19:07.876 --> 01:19:09.578
I really care about what I want to do

01:19:09.978 --> 01:19:12.714
If it's something I've figured out I want to do

01:19:12.714 --> 01:19:14.216
Don't try to stop me

01:19:14.883 --> 01:19:17.752
And I'll definitely do my absolute best

01:19:18.053 --> 01:19:19.588
But if it's something I don't want to do

01:19:19.988 --> 01:19:22.757
Forcing me won't help, I won't do it. Right

01:19:22.791 --> 01:19:24.292
Are you very competitive?

01:19:24.392 --> 01:19:25.193
Pretty strong

01:19:26.561 --> 01:19:29.998
Yeah, but I think I'm more competing with myself

01:19:29.998 --> 01:19:31.166
pushing myself, I guess

01:19:31.600 --> 01:19:33.869
Not really willing to compete with others

01:19:34.136 --> 01:19:34.936
Oh, right

01:19:35.370 --> 01:19:36.338
Of course, if

01:19:36.371 --> 01:19:37.439
well

01:19:37.439 --> 01:19:39.241
it's something I think is important

01:19:39.241 --> 01:19:40.275
and you also think it's important

01:19:40.275 --> 01:19:44.146
then I definitely have to outdo you, hehe

01:19:44.813 --> 01:19:47.182
So then you got to Tsinghua, that was even more amazing

01:19:47.182 --> 01:19:48.950
You studied quantum physics, why?

01:19:49.384 --> 01:19:53.555
Yeah, I was doing condensed matter theory at the time

01:19:53.588 --> 01:19:54.623
Why did you choose this major?

01:19:56.424 --> 01:19:57.526
A twist of fate

01:19:57.826 --> 01:19:59.628
Looking back now

01:19:59.628 --> 01:20:00.595
Of course I can

01:20:00.929 --> 01:20:04.032
come up with some very reasonable-sounding explanations

01:20:04.199 --> 01:20:06.134
But honestly, going back to that time

01:20:06.134 --> 01:20:07.435
I think it was just a twist of fate

01:20:08.703 --> 01:20:11.106
So at that time we were in the Jixian class

01:20:11.106 --> 01:20:13.141
And the Jixian class had a very good tradition

01:20:13.141 --> 01:20:15.844
First of all, although the Jixian class was in the physics department

01:20:15.844 --> 01:20:17.412
It didn't restrict what students could do

01:20:17.746 --> 01:20:19.848
Actually 2/3 of the students in the Jixian class wouldn't do physics

01:20:20.115 --> 01:20:20.615
Ah

01:20:20.916 --> 01:20:21.550
And for

01:20:21.550 --> 01:20:22.684
Why did you enter this class

01:20:23.151 --> 01:20:23.919
Uh

01:20:24.886 --> 01:20:26.755
At that time the entire Tsinghua physics department was Jixian class

01:20:27.022 --> 01:20:28.156
Maybe not anymore now

01:20:28.190 --> 01:20:29.057
Anyway it was at that time

01:20:29.057 --> 01:20:29.925
And another good tradition it had was

01:20:29.925 --> 01:20:32.761
It encouraged students to learn through practice

01:20:33.028 --> 01:20:33.929
So it encouraged students

01:20:33.929 --> 01:20:36.731
To enter research labs as early as possible

01:20:37.199 --> 01:20:40.535
And learn through research

01:20:40.902 --> 01:20:45.507
And at that time I really wanted to do theory

01:20:46.241 --> 01:20:47.642
Was it because you found it difficult

01:20:48.844 --> 01:20:51.613
It feels like you have a fascination with difficulty

01:20:52.614 --> 01:20:53.782
Maybe it's also a kind of illness

01:20:54.049 --> 01:20:56.151
I can talk more about this later

01:20:56.151 --> 01:20:57.385
What are the bad consequences of this illness

01:20:57.385 --> 01:20:58.520
Hahaha

01:20:58.520 --> 01:21:00.689
Right and then then right

01:21:00.689 --> 01:21:01.890
Then I wanted to do theory

01:21:01.890 --> 01:21:04.459
And of course the Jixian class

01:21:04.459 --> 01:21:05.460
Or what we call the Xuetang class

01:21:05.460 --> 01:21:06.528
Had a smaller class

01:21:06.528 --> 01:21:07.963
And then the

01:21:07.963 --> 01:21:09.130
Teacher recommended saying hey

01:21:09.130 --> 01:21:10.699
The Institute for Advanced Study is a great place

01:21:10.699 --> 01:21:11.466
Tsinghua Institute for Advanced Study

01:21:11.466 --> 01:21:13.668
The research institute founded by Mr. Chen-Ning Yang

01:21:13.668 --> 01:21:14.502
Is a great place

01:21:14.603 --> 01:21:16.171
So I went there to find a teacher

01:21:16.805 --> 01:21:19.407
And there happened to be

01:21:19.407 --> 01:21:21.109
A teacher who was still young at that time called

01:21:21.109 --> 01:21:22.577
Called Wang Zhong, he was my undergraduate teacher

01:21:22.577 --> 01:21:24.279
Mm-hmm, at that time he didn't have many students either

01:21:24.779 --> 01:21:27.315
And we chatted

01:21:27.315 --> 01:21:28.483
Of course I knew nothing

01:21:28.516 --> 01:21:29.584
But he was quite patient

01:21:29.584 --> 01:21:30.719
And gave me

01:21:30.719 --> 01:21:32.887
Gave me some papers to read

01:21:32.954 --> 01:21:34.155
And after reading I discussed with him

01:21:34.155 --> 01:21:36.858
Later I discovered condensed matter theory

01:21:36.858 --> 01:21:37.959
Especially the project we were doing at that time

01:21:37.959 --> 01:21:39.561
Was related to topological insulators

01:21:39.561 --> 01:21:42.931
And these kinds of directions

01:21:42.931 --> 01:21:44.132
Actually

01:21:44.132 --> 01:21:46.801
Was a direction very suitable for undergraduates to get started with

01:21:47.068 --> 01:21:49.504
It didn't require too much background knowledge

01:21:50.205 --> 01:21:51.907
You only needed to know

01:21:52.007 --> 01:21:54.542
The most basic thing is you need to know quantum mechanics

01:21:54.542 --> 01:21:55.277
Statistical mechanics

01:21:55.277 --> 01:21:56.344
Solid state physics

01:21:56.478 --> 01:21:59.080
Which are actually very very easy to learn

01:21:59.080 --> 01:22:00.515
Basic knowledge

01:22:00.515 --> 01:22:01.483
But it might really test

01:22:01.483 --> 01:22:02.917
The depth of your understanding of this knowledge

01:22:03.918 --> 01:22:05.053
So for undergraduates

01:22:05.053 --> 01:22:07.589
It's actually a particularly good direction

01:22:07.689 --> 01:22:09.524
Where you can get started quickly

01:22:09.524 --> 01:22:10.659
And do some actual projects

01:22:10.659 --> 01:22:13.762
And then we did some work together

01:22:13.762 --> 01:22:14.763
Among which possibly

01:22:14.763 --> 01:22:17.532
The work in open quantum systems

01:22:17.799 --> 01:22:20.602
Looking back now is still quite important work

01:22:20.602 --> 01:22:23.138
Right and then

01:22:23.505 --> 01:22:24.606
In a sense

01:22:24.606 --> 01:22:27.409
I think looking back now

01:22:27.409 --> 01:22:28.743
Doing that work

01:22:28.743 --> 01:22:30.445
Doing research during that period

01:22:30.445 --> 01:22:32.080
Is actually very very similar to doing AI now

01:22:32.914 --> 01:22:34.949
It's more that you have an idea

01:22:34.949 --> 01:22:35.884
You have an understanding

01:22:36.084 --> 01:22:38.086
And at that stage you can

01:22:38.086 --> 01:22:39.654
You can do a numerical experiment

01:22:39.754 --> 01:22:41.790
To verify whether your idea and understanding are correct

01:22:42.757 --> 01:22:44.259
You find AI is actually the same

01:22:44.326 --> 01:22:45.560
AI is also you have an idea

01:22:45.560 --> 01:22:46.461
You have an understanding

01:22:46.528 --> 01:22:48.063
You design some experiments

01:22:48.063 --> 01:22:49.597
To verify whether your understanding is correct

01:22:49.798 --> 01:22:52.734
And then you design some model

01:22:52.734 --> 01:22:53.735
Training pipeline

01:22:53.735 --> 01:22:56.504
To implement your ideas

01:22:56.671 --> 01:22:58.974
Right so actually these two are very similar

01:23:00.709 --> 01:23:03.545
Can you talk about your non-Hermitian system research

01:23:04.412 --> 01:23:05.814
Ah, I can talk about it

01:23:05.814 --> 01:23:07.082
I'll try to speak in human terms

01:23:07.816 --> 01:23:09.751
But it's also possible I'll actually be talking nonsense

01:23:09.751 --> 01:23:12.754
So those who don't want to listen can skip ahead

01:23:12.754 --> 01:23:14.556
Hahahaha

01:23:14.556 --> 01:23:16.091
Slide the progress bar

01:23:16.391 --> 01:23:18.393
You can set two markers on the progress bar

01:23:18.393 --> 01:23:19.894
Right and then right

01:23:19.894 --> 01:23:21.396
Non-Hermitian systems are like this

01:23:22.364 --> 01:23:26.201
One of the most basic assumptions of quantum mechanics is

01:23:26.201 --> 01:23:27.736
An isolated system

01:23:27.736 --> 01:23:31.373
Its evolution is described by unitary evolution

01:23:32.407 --> 01:23:33.375
Unitary evolution is kind of nonsense

01:23:33.375 --> 01:23:34.509
Sorry

01:23:34.509 --> 01:23:35.610
What unitary evolution means is

01:23:35.610 --> 01:23:37.946
It's a linear process

01:23:37.946 --> 01:23:40.348
And this linear process

01:23:40.348 --> 01:23:42.717
Can be described by an operator

01:23:42.717 --> 01:23:44.519
Called the Hamiltonian

01:23:44.819 --> 01:23:47.021
Ah, the Hamiltonian, in a certain sense

01:23:47.088 --> 01:23:48.890
It's somewhat like the energy of the system

01:23:48.890 --> 01:23:49.758
But not exactly

01:23:49.758 --> 01:23:50.725
It's somewhat analogous to

01:23:50.725 --> 01:23:53.294
So
It determines how the system evolves over time

01:23:53.595 --> 01:23:54.429
And if it's

01:23:54.429 --> 01:23:55.130
An isolated system

01:23:55.130 --> 01:23:57.132
This Hamiltonian will be a Hermitian matrix

01:23:57.365 --> 01:23:59.100
A Hermitian matrix is one where you transpose it

01:23:59.100 --> 01:24:00.435
And then take the complex conjugate

01:24:00.435 --> 01:24:01.503
And it's the same as the original

01:24:02.670 --> 01:24:04.739
But real systems

01:24:04.739 --> 01:24:06.808
The vast majority are not isolated systems

01:24:07.308 --> 01:24:08.843
For example, you

01:24:08.843 --> 01:24:09.644
Me, as a human being

01:24:09.644 --> 01:24:11.212
Definitely have to exchange information with the outside world

01:24:11.212 --> 01:24:12.280
And exchange matter

01:24:12.680 --> 01:24:14.215
Materials are the same

01:24:14.215 --> 01:24:16.684
If you put a piece of material there

01:24:17.419 --> 01:24:19.020
Unless you create an extremely high vacuum

01:24:19.587 --> 01:24:21.189
You always have to interact with the substrate

01:24:21.322 --> 01:24:22.957
You have to exchange with the external environment

01:24:23.158 --> 01:24:24.092
So real systems

01:24:24.092 --> 01:24:25.593
Are mostly not isolated systems

01:24:25.760 --> 01:24:26.394
And isolated systems

01:24:26.394 --> 01:24:29.130
Won't be described by a unitary process

01:24:29.397 --> 01:24:30.799
And the corresponding Hamiltonian

01:24:30.799 --> 01:24:31.933
Won't be Hermitian either

01:24:31.933 --> 01:24:32.734
Hamiltonian

01:24:32.767 --> 01:24:34.436
That's where the term 'non-Hermitian' comes from

01:24:34.436 --> 01:24:36.738
It's essentially for studying open quantum systems

01:24:36.905 --> 01:24:38.706
Quantum systems that exchange with the outside world

01:24:38.706 --> 01:24:39.507
Their behavior

01:24:39.941 --> 01:24:43.344
And at that time, something very puzzling was discovered

01:24:43.344 --> 01:24:45.547
We were initially trying to study

01:24:45.547 --> 01:24:47.949
Some topological phenomena in these open quantum systems

01:24:48.950 --> 01:24:50.118
And then we found

01:24:50.118 --> 01:24:52.220
The theoretical results from hand calculations

01:24:52.420 --> 01:24:56.724
Just couldn't match the numerical results no matter what

01:24:57.659 --> 01:24:58.226
More precisely

01:24:58.226 --> 01:24:59.194
The hand calculation result

01:24:59.194 --> 01:25:00.295
Assumed the system

01:25:00.295 --> 01:25:01.296
Had periodic boundary conditions

01:25:01.296 --> 01:25:02.597
For example, on a ring

01:25:02.597 --> 01:25:04.632
Or on the surface of a torus

01:25:04.899 --> 01:25:06.835
And numerically

01:25:07.068 --> 01:25:08.870
Because it's closer to the actual situation

01:25:08.870 --> 01:25:11.039
It would calculate with open boundaries

01:25:11.039 --> 01:25:13.274
For example, the behavior of a material in a square shape

01:25:13.274 --> 01:25:15.043
And these two results just couldn't be reconciled

01:25:15.410 --> 01:25:16.744
So we tried to understand this

01:25:16.878 --> 01:25:18.079
And later found

01:25:18.079 --> 01:25:20.849
The basic paradigm people used to describe Hermitian systems

01:25:20.849 --> 01:25:24.185
A fundamental paradigm

01:25:24.219 --> 01:25:26.354
Is the so-called Bloch wave

01:25:26.421 --> 01:25:28.423
Which assumes the eigenstates of the system are

01:25:28.423 --> 01:25:30.625
Linear combinations of waves

01:25:31.226 --> 01:25:32.894
This

01:25:32.894 --> 01:25:33.995
Sine and cosine waves, that kind of thing

01:25:33.995 --> 01:25:34.896
Linear combinations of such waves

01:25:34.896 --> 01:25:38.233
This assumption

01:25:38.666 --> 01:25:43.104
In non-Hermitian systems, it actually

01:25:43.104 --> 01:25:45.206
breaks down — it becomes wrong

01:25:45.306 --> 01:25:46.508
The fact is

01:25:46.574 --> 01:25:47.208
Later we found

01:25:47.208 --> 01:25:48.076
In non-Hermitian systems

01:25:48.076 --> 01:25:50.078
Actually, the energy eigenstates

01:25:50.078 --> 01:25:50.879
All

01:25:51.012 --> 01:25:53.248
Can potentially accumulate at one edge of the system

01:25:53.481 --> 01:25:55.683
Right, and then we systematically established this

01:25:55.683 --> 01:25:57.018
Set of descriptive methods

01:25:57.152 --> 01:26:00.622
And then built a framework

01:26:00.622 --> 01:26:03.625
To describe a non-Hermitian system with open boundaries

01:26:03.691 --> 01:26:05.460
How to describe its eigenstates

01:26:05.460 --> 01:26:07.695
And thereby describe its time evolution

01:26:07.695 --> 01:26:08.596
And some dynamics

01:26:09.197 --> 01:26:10.498
So

01:26:10.498 --> 01:26:12.367
That was the work at that time

01:26:12.367 --> 01:26:15.303
And later there was a lot of

01:26:15.670 --> 01:26:17.605
Because it was actually a

01:26:17.605 --> 01:26:18.840
A paradigm shift

01:26:18.840 --> 01:26:20.575
So later there was a lot of

01:26:20.575 --> 01:26:21.776
Follow-up work

01:26:21.776 --> 01:26:22.877
But later I actually switched directions

01:26:22.877 --> 01:26:25.013
So I didn't continue much in this direction

01:26:25.980 --> 01:26:27.048
Why didn't you continue with it

01:26:28.149 --> 01:26:30.985
It's hard to catch a paradigm shift, isn't it

01:26:31.886 --> 01:26:33.688
It's hard to catch a paradigm shift

01:26:33.688 --> 01:26:35.223
Yes, yes

01:26:35.490 --> 01:26:37.058
This is the weakness of human nature

01:26:37.058 --> 01:26:38.092
I feel like

01:26:38.092 --> 01:26:40.828
I always love challenging myself with things I don't know

01:26:40.828 --> 01:26:42.664
Hahaha especially at that time

01:26:42.664 --> 01:26:43.198
Just

01:26:43.198 --> 01:26:46.000
I don't know what I was feeling in that direction

01:26:46.034 --> 01:26:49.837
Maybe looking back at that work a few years later

01:26:49.837 --> 01:26:51.573
It would become the most important work in that direction

01:26:52.073 --> 01:26:53.408
Later when you do some more work

01:26:53.408 --> 01:26:55.243
It might indeed make you more famous

01:26:55.310 --> 01:26:56.844
Get more citations

01:26:56.844 --> 01:26:58.446
Write more good journal articles

01:26:58.446 --> 01:26:59.347
Find a good faculty position

01:26:59.347 --> 01:27:03.351
But it feels like for a scientific career

01:27:03.351 --> 01:27:05.887
It wouldn't be that exciting

01:27:06.187 --> 01:27:08.156
So at that time I wanted to switch to something else

01:27:08.189 --> 01:27:08.990
Switch to something I wasn't good at

01:27:08.990 --> 01:27:10.291
Do it right

01:27:10.291 --> 01:27:10.692
And then

01:27:10.692 --> 01:27:12.627
So when doing my PhD I switched directions

01:27:12.627 --> 01:27:14.195
To do high energy theory

01:27:14.596 --> 01:27:15.129
High energy theory, right

01:27:15.296 --> 01:27:16.397
High energy physics, right

01:27:16.898 --> 01:27:18.900
So your undergraduate and PhD were also different

01:27:18.967 --> 01:27:19.801
Also different

01:27:20.068 --> 01:27:22.804
