02:49:34.964 --> 02:49:36.533
But I think in the end it became a two-way street

02:49:36.533 --> 02:49:37.767
So, hahaha

02:49:37.767 --> 02:49:39.135
Wasn't OpenAI an option for you back then?

02:49:39.135 --> 02:49:41.137
If you wanted to leave Anthropic

02:49:41.271 --> 02:49:42.839
OpenAI was also an option at the time

02:49:42.839 --> 02:49:44.641
OpenAI should still have been stronger than Gemini

02:49:44.641 --> 02:49:46.643
In terms of momentum, right?

02:49:46.643 --> 02:49:47.510
At that time

02:49:47.644 --> 02:49:48.912
But

02:49:48.912 --> 02:49:49.512
Though back then

02:49:49.512 --> 02:49:52.448
Weren't there all those internal politics

02:49:52.448 --> 02:49:53.750
Infighting was starting to emerge

02:49:53.750 --> 02:49:55.585
I think so

02:49:55.585 --> 02:49:57.487
So OpenAI was indeed an option for me back then

02:49:57.487 --> 02:49:59.756
And of course there were also options like xAI

02:49:59.756 --> 02:50:00.790
And I think

02:50:00.790 --> 02:50:02.258
The main reason I didn't end up at OpenAI

02:50:02.258 --> 02:50:03.927
Was that I had concerns about its

02:50:03.927 --> 02:50:06.496
Culture, at least at that time

02:50:06.496 --> 02:50:09.065
I had pretty big concerns about its culture

02:50:09.699 --> 02:50:11.868
I just felt that

02:50:13.336 --> 02:50:16.072
To put it bluntly, people who actually get things done

02:50:16.105 --> 02:50:18.141
There weren't as many as at Gemini

02:50:18.641 --> 02:50:20.276
Even fewer than at Anthropic

02:50:20.376 --> 02:50:22.645
Right? I really care about that

02:50:22.645 --> 02:50:24.314
Hahaha, yeah

02:50:24.314 --> 02:50:26.583
So a sense of cultural and personal connection brought you to Gemini

02:50:26.583 --> 02:50:27.951
Yeah

02:50:28.451 --> 02:50:31.688
And then you also caught that Gemini 3 inflection point, right?

02:50:31.688 --> 02:50:32.088
Hmm

02:50:32.088 --> 02:50:36.259
Gemini 3 should have been a major turning point for them

02:50:36.259 --> 02:50:37.760
A turning point period, right?

02:50:37.760 --> 02:50:40.530
I think in terms of actual impact

02:50:40.530 --> 02:50:41.998
I think it was two things

02:50:41.998 --> 02:50:45.134
That created a major turning point for Gemini

02:50:45.134 --> 02:50:48.671
Turning it into a heavyweight

02:50:48.671 --> 02:50:49.372
player in the market

02:50:49.372 --> 02:50:51.541
The player is Nano Banana

02:50:51.541 --> 02:50:52.375
Nano Banana and Gemini 3

02:50:52.375 --> 02:50:55.445
Two things back to back, which is

02:50:55.445 --> 02:50:57.146
I think if there were only Gemini 3

02:50:57.146 --> 02:50:59.315
It probably wouldn't have had such great results

02:50:59.315 --> 02:51:01.384
Because when your market share is less than

02:51:01.384 --> 02:51:03.052
Even 10%

02:51:03.119 --> 02:51:04.587
Whether your model is slightly better or worse

02:51:04.587 --> 02:51:08.758
It just spreads too slowly

02:51:08.858 --> 02:51:10.393
But what Nano Banana did was

02:51:10.393 --> 02:51:13.062
First, it went viral in the market, it was a huge hit

02:51:13.162 --> 02:51:15.598
Then a ton of people downloaded the Gemini app

02:51:16.099 --> 02:51:18.534
And then Gemini 3 was released right after

02:51:18.534 --> 02:51:19.969
Retaining those users

02:51:20.637 --> 02:51:21.070
So

02:51:21.070 --> 02:51:23.039
Now it's become a major player

02:51:23.339 --> 02:51:25.642
I think if Gemini hadn't thrown this punch

02:51:25.642 --> 02:51:28.077
OpenAI's position would be really comfortable

02:51:28.077 --> 02:51:29.545
Its market share is so high that

02:51:29.545 --> 02:51:30.980
Whatever you do with the model

02:51:30.980 --> 02:51:34.917
It doesn't actually matter that much to them

02:51:35.084 --> 02:51:36.386
To be honest

02:51:36.386 --> 02:51:39.689
I think when ordinary people use models

02:51:39.689 --> 02:51:42.659
Their perception of the model's capabilities

02:51:42.659 --> 02:51:44.494
Is actually very, very weak

02:51:45.528 --> 02:51:47.263
Most people don't even use the o-series models

02:51:47.263 --> 02:51:48.998
Most people just use the regular

02:51:48.998 --> 02:51:50.099
ChatGPT one

02:51:50.300 --> 02:51:52.869
Right, so I think for Genimi

02:51:52.869 --> 02:51:56.539
This Nano Banana built up the user volume

02:51:56.539 --> 02:51:57.974
And then Gemini 3 retained those users

02:51:57.974 --> 02:51:59.575
Was something critical

02:52:00.309 --> 02:52:03.513
How many ChatGPT users did it actually take away?

02:52:03.846 --> 02:52:07.450
Hmm, I don't know the exact numbers now

02:52:07.517 --> 02:52:10.520
But my feeling is

02:52:10.520 --> 02:52:13.956
Gemini's market share is probably around 20%

02:52:15.224 --> 02:52:18.594
But I haven't really checked the current data carefully

02:52:19.095 --> 02:52:21.431
Looking at it with hindsight

02:52:21.431 --> 02:52:22.632
These two factors

02:52:22.632 --> 02:52:25.735
Together contributed to Gemini's challenge to OpenAI today

02:52:25.735 --> 02:52:28.738
So from an insider's perspective you must have known earlier

02:52:28.738 --> 02:52:30.606
What happened and why

02:52:30.807 --> 02:52:32.842
Google would undergo such changes

02:52:32.842 --> 02:52:34.143
Yeah, I think

02:52:34.143 --> 02:52:37.146
First of all, Google's technical reserves

02:52:37.146 --> 02:52:37.880
Have always been sufficient

02:52:37.880 --> 02:52:38.981
Hmm, enough talent

02:52:38.981 --> 02:52:40.483
Yeah, they've always been sufficient

02:52:40.483 --> 02:52:42.051
And then

02:52:42.051 --> 02:52:42.785
Organizationally speaking

02:52:42.785 --> 02:52:44.387
It became increasingly clear later on

02:52:44.687 --> 02:52:48.558
It's having a better framework to let

02:52:48.558 --> 02:52:50.293
Everyone work together on this thing

02:52:50.493 --> 02:52:53.196
So there might slowly be some progress

02:52:53.463 --> 02:52:55.965
Right and then

02:52:56.933 --> 02:52:58.267
I think in a sense

02:52:58.267 --> 02:52:59.602
As an outsider

02:53:00.370 --> 02:53:01.437
In a sense

02:53:01.437 --> 02:53:03.539
I think OpenAI saved Google's life

02:53:04.240 --> 02:53:08.745
Oh because everyone used to worry

02:53:08.745 --> 02:53:09.579
This chatbot

02:53:09.579 --> 02:53:11.447
Would completely replace search

02:53:11.447 --> 02:53:13.015
Right if this really happened

02:53:13.015 --> 02:53:14.250
Google would actually be in a tough spot

02:53:14.817 --> 02:53:15.885
But fortunately

02:53:15.885 --> 02:53:18.755
OpenAI did this thing first

02:53:19.455 --> 02:53:21.791
Then made Google realize this thing is important

02:53:21.991 --> 02:53:23.860
But it didn't take this thing all the way

02:53:23.993 --> 02:53:25.461
Didn't take this thing to the extreme

02:53:25.828 --> 02:53:28.564
Didn't completely kill off search

02:53:28.698 --> 02:53:30.199
Maybe just ate some market share

02:53:30.800 --> 02:53:31.667
As a result

02:53:31.667 --> 02:53:34.470
Let Google itself catch up on chatbots too

02:53:34.470 --> 02:53:36.005
Now the one in a tough spot is them

02:53:37.473 --> 02:53:38.241
What if

02:53:38.241 --> 02:53:41.244
For example there's a company, just hypothetically

02:53:41.244 --> 02:53:42.845
In a fictional world

02:53:42.845 --> 02:53:44.781
A company not only made a chatbot

02:53:44.981 --> 02:53:46.215
But also marched forward triumphantly

02:53:46.215 --> 02:53:47.083
Doing better and better

02:53:47.083 --> 02:53:50.353
Really just ate up your search in one go

02:53:50.353 --> 02:53:51.854
Completely didn't give you a chance to fight back

02:53:51.988 --> 02:53:53.222
Then it would be very tough

02:53:53.256 --> 02:53:54.690
Did the chatbot not eat up search

02:53:54.690 --> 02:53:56.392
Because OpenAI didn't do it well

02:53:56.492 --> 02:53:57.226
Or why

02:53:57.226 --> 02:53:59.262
Or because it can't kill off search

02:53:59.495 --> 02:54:00.663
I think

02:54:00.663 --> 02:54:01.631
Both sides actually have reasons

02:54:01.631 --> 02:54:03.833
That is first um

02:54:03.833 --> 02:54:05.468
Current chatbot interaction methods

02:54:05.468 --> 02:54:07.970
Actually won't completely eat up search

02:54:08.171 --> 02:54:10.606
Because it's stronger than search

02:54:10.606 --> 02:54:12.375
Like we said earliest just now

02:54:12.375 --> 02:54:13.843
The one point it's stronger than search

02:54:13.843 --> 02:54:15.444
Is that it has strong interactivity

02:54:15.444 --> 02:54:16.479
You can follow up

02:54:16.546 --> 02:54:17.980
And

02:54:18.147 --> 02:54:20.616
It can help you condense some very complex information

02:54:21.050 --> 02:54:22.084
This is where it's very strong

02:54:22.084 --> 02:54:24.353
So this portion of usage scenarios

02:54:24.353 --> 02:54:27.423
It will indeed steal people from search but

02:54:27.423 --> 02:54:28.891
There are still some very stupid scenarios in search

02:54:28.891 --> 02:54:30.359
Where you have a very simple thing

02:54:31.027 --> 02:54:32.595
You don't want to waste this time

02:54:32.595 --> 02:54:33.963
On a chatbot

02:54:33.963 --> 02:54:34.797
Like I just

02:54:35.064 --> 02:54:37.400
I just search buy rice

02:54:37.867 --> 02:54:39.802
I search buy and it's done

02:54:39.936 --> 02:54:40.536
Just

02:54:40.536 --> 02:54:41.938
Do I have to ask ChatGPT

02:54:41.938 --> 02:54:43.539
Do I have to ask which one is good

02:54:43.539 --> 02:54:44.974
And it's still spinning there

02:54:44.974 --> 02:54:46.075
Spinning for half a day

02:54:46.075 --> 02:54:46.642
Then gives you a link

02:54:46.642 --> 02:54:47.276
You click again

02:54:47.276 --> 02:54:48.578
Then go to the webpage to buy

02:54:48.611 --> 02:54:49.679
Right there's no need for that

02:54:49.779 --> 02:54:52.381
So from actual usage

02:54:52.448 --> 02:54:53.216
Its current form

02:54:53.216 --> 02:54:55.485
Is not enough to completely eat up search

02:54:55.685 --> 02:54:57.753
Right and

02:54:58.855 --> 02:55:00.089
Of course from another perspective

02:55:00.089 --> 02:55:01.757
It might not have reached the peak in the chatbot thing either

02:55:01.757 --> 02:55:03.192
It really let Google catch up

02:55:03.526 --> 02:55:06.562
Now it's not quite caught up yet

02:55:06.562 --> 02:55:08.664
In terms of product

02:55:08.998 --> 02:55:09.765
I think in terms of product it's not caught up

02:55:09.765 --> 02:55:11.934
But in terms of model it has already caught up

02:55:11.934 --> 02:55:14.937
But if you want investors to invest in OpenAI

02:55:15.638 --> 02:55:18.040
They would say

02:55:18.040 --> 02:55:19.041
When they placed their bet

02:55:19.675 --> 02:55:21.010
They recognized clearly

02:55:21.010 --> 02:55:22.478
OpenAI is actually a product company

02:55:22.478 --> 02:55:24.547
Its moat is actually product and brand

02:55:24.614 --> 02:55:27.250
Then from today's perspective

02:55:27.250 --> 02:55:29.385
It seems Google hasn't been able to in this matter

02:55:29.385 --> 02:55:30.987
Catch up

02:55:32.121 --> 02:55:33.256
Can't say surpass OpenAI

02:55:34.323 --> 02:55:35.591
Catch up to OpenAI

02:55:35.591 --> 02:55:36.726
Right I think

02:55:37.493 --> 02:55:38.995
This is actually

02:55:39.862 --> 02:55:40.763
Anyway this is all from my perspective as an outsider

02:55:41.697 --> 02:55:43.065
An observer's perspective

02:55:43.065 --> 02:55:44.333
You're a commentator today

02:55:44.333 --> 02:55:45.701
Hahaha

02:55:45.701 --> 02:55:47.503
From an observer's perspective

02:55:47.503 --> 02:55:49.338
I think Google has traditionally been a bit slow with products

02:55:49.338 --> 02:55:53.609
Has always been relatively slow

02:55:53.609 --> 02:55:55.478
And so

02:55:55.478 --> 02:55:57.213
然后所以

02:55:57.213 --> 02:55:59.615
Do you think OpenAI has an advantage when it comes to products?

02:55:59.615 --> 02:56:00.983
I think it's possible.

02:56:01.083 --> 02:56:01.384
Right.

02:56:01.384 --> 02:56:04.887
And what's one thing Google is particularly good at?

02:56:04.887 --> 02:56:08.691
Finding an extremely simple product form.

02:56:08.691 --> 02:56:10.826
Everyone looks the same.

02:56:10.826 --> 02:56:12.361
Then it just competes with you relentlessly on technology.

02:56:13.129 --> 02:56:14.130
And you can't outcompete it.

02:56:15.197 --> 02:56:16.332
Oh, right.

02:56:16.599 --> 02:56:18.134
That's exactly what Google is good at.

02:56:18.734 --> 02:56:21.671
Because search engines are exactly like that.

02:56:21.871 --> 02:56:22.805
Search is a classic example.

02:56:22.805 --> 02:56:24.073
Everyone has the same search box.

02:56:24.073 --> 02:56:26.108
One button, but it just searches faster than you.

02:56:26.108 --> 02:56:26.809
And more accurately than you.

02:56:26.809 --> 02:56:28.244
There's nothing you can do about it.

02:56:28.244 --> 02:56:29.679
Mm-hmm.

02:56:30.813 --> 02:56:31.614
So that's why.

02:56:31.614 --> 02:56:32.415
Like.

02:56:33.282 --> 02:56:34.684
It feels like all along.

02:56:35.051 --> 02:56:38.688
Google has been in this state of doing very well, but...

02:56:40.189 --> 02:56:41.791
Wall Street never really bought into it.

02:56:42.592 --> 02:56:45.261
Everyone always wondered where this company's moat really is.

02:56:45.728 --> 02:56:47.663
There's no product ingenuity.

02:56:47.663 --> 02:56:49.131
No retention mechanisms either.

02:56:49.832 --> 02:56:51.434
But it has survived until now.

02:56:51.667 --> 02:56:53.102
So what's the reason its technology is so good?

02:56:53.603 --> 02:56:54.837
I think it's still about the people, right?

02:56:54.837 --> 02:56:56.172
I think it's the culture.

02:56:56.305 --> 02:56:58.240
It's said to be.

02:56:58.240 --> 02:56:59.442
A place that particularly, particularly values.

02:56:59.909 --> 02:57:01.510
In the past, it particularly valued engineers.

02:57:01.510 --> 02:57:03.112
Later, it particularly valued research.

02:57:03.112 --> 02:57:03.913
That's the kind of culture.

02:57:05.214 --> 02:57:05.982
So it's very well suited for.

02:57:05.982 --> 02:57:07.917
Products where technological capability spills over.

02:57:07.917 --> 02:57:09.518
Capability-based products.

02:57:09.518 --> 02:57:11.520
Right, if you look at it from this angle.

02:57:11.520 --> 02:57:13.689
Then do you think OpenAI's position is secure?

02:57:13.689 --> 02:57:14.490
Now?

02:57:15.124 --> 02:57:16.592
I don't think anyone's position is secure right now.

02:57:16.592 --> 02:57:19.095
Hahahaha, right.

02:57:19.095 --> 02:57:23.032
I think the form of AI.

02:57:23.032 --> 02:57:25.534
Still has a long way to go.

02:57:25.534 --> 02:57:26.335
Mm-hmm.

02:57:26.636 --> 02:57:29.505
We're not at any endgame yet.

02:57:29.505 --> 02:57:31.574
That's the feeling about this.

02:57:32.174 --> 02:57:32.975
Right.

02:57:33.876 --> 02:57:35.811
It feels like back home there's already a bit of this sentiment.

02:57:36.112 --> 02:57:36.912
Yeah, I don't get it.

02:57:36.912 --> 02:57:38.214
Like, why don't I get it?

02:57:38.214 --> 02:57:38.781
I'm really puzzled.

02:57:38.781 --> 02:57:39.181
Like.

02:57:39.181 --> 02:57:41.584
So back home, people think we're fighting over a super app.

02:57:42.018 --> 02:57:44.420
A super app is zero-sum, right?

02:57:44.587 --> 02:57:49.325
I think conditioned on the chatbot thing (taking the chatbot

02:57:49.325 --> 02:57:50.626
as the condition to build on) that's the super app.

02:57:51.093 --> 02:57:54.330
Then maybe there's something to fight over.

02:57:54.463 --> 02:57:55.765
But the problem is.

02:57:55.765 --> 02:57:58.367
Is this form the super app form?

02:57:58.734 --> 02:57:59.602
What if someone else.

02:57:59.602 --> 02:58:01.671
Comes out with a completely different form one day.

02:58:01.771 --> 02:58:04.173
And your functionality becomes a subset.

02:58:04.173 --> 02:58:04.974
Of that thing.

02:58:05.775 --> 02:58:06.876
That's quite possible, right?

02:58:06.876 --> 02:58:08.377
I don't think there's anything.

02:58:08.377 --> 02:58:10.846
I don't see anything impossible.

02:58:11.447 --> 02:58:13.449
Why wouldn't the chatbot be the ultimate form?

02:58:13.449 --> 02:58:15.584
But after all these years, this is all we've seen.

02:58:16.685 --> 02:58:18.621
Right, it's all just a chat box.

02:58:18.621 --> 02:58:21.057
I think on this matter, I really don't have any.

02:58:21.190 --> 02:58:24.026
Rational or quantitative criteria.

02:58:24.026 --> 02:58:24.994
To explain it.

02:58:24.994 --> 02:58:27.596
More like you just feel like this whole thing is stupid.

02:58:27.596 --> 02:58:29.699
Like this model clearly has so many capabilities.

02:58:30.066 --> 02:58:31.801
But the way we use it is a chatbot (Note: This video was recorded over 2 months ago, when the agent paradigm was not yet clear).

02:58:32.935 --> 02:58:34.804
It just doesn't quite make sense.

02:58:34.804 --> 02:58:35.471
You know what I mean, so.

02:58:35.471 --> 02:58:36.839
We need a product manager.

02:58:36.839 --> 02:58:38.874
To unlock the model's capabilities.

02:58:39.175 --> 02:58:41.210
Hahaha.

02:58:41.710 --> 02:58:44.647
Humans have only communicated with AI through chatbots until now.

02:58:44.647 --> 02:58:45.781
That seems stupid to you, right?

02:58:45.915 --> 02:58:46.782
It's stupid because.

02:58:46.782 --> 02:58:48.584
Then what should we use to communicate with AI?

02:58:48.584 --> 02:58:48.984
Haven't figured it out.

02:58:48.984 --> 02:58:50.119
If I had figured it out, I'd already be doing it.

02:58:50.186 --> 02:58:53.255
Hahahaha.

02:58:53.255 --> 02:58:53.889
Hey, you didn't tell me.

02:58:53.889 --> 02:58:55.658
What exactly changed inside Google.

02:58:55.658 --> 02:58:57.793
To lead to what the outside world saw.

02:58:57.793 --> 02:58:59.395
The rapid leap in model capabilities.

02:58:59.628 --> 02:59:00.729
Right, like I just said, it's one thing.

02:59:00.729 --> 02:59:01.931
I think the organization has more clarity now.

02:59:01.931 --> 02:59:03.099
And.

02:59:03.099 --> 02:59:04.266
Once the organization is clear.

02:59:04.266 --> 02:59:05.668
Did the organization change?

02:59:06.202 --> 02:59:06.702
Right.

02:59:06.702 --> 02:59:08.003
Especially pre-training.

02:59:08.003 --> 02:59:09.572
Has become very, very clear now.

02:59:09.839 --> 02:59:12.007
That is who is responsible for what

02:59:12.041 --> 02:59:13.008
And every point

02:59:13.008 --> 02:59:15.511
Who is the responsible person at every node

02:59:15.511 --> 02:59:16.378
These things are very clear

02:59:16.378 --> 02:59:17.613
Was it chaotic before

02:59:17.613 --> 02:59:19.615
It was very chaotic in the earliest days

02:59:19.615 --> 02:59:21.117
I wasn't there in the earliest days

02:59:21.117 --> 02:59:22.651
But according to colleagues

02:59:22.651 --> 02:59:25.020
Based on colleagues' or people I knew's descriptions

02:59:25.020 --> 02:59:26.122
It was still more chaotic before

02:59:26.122 --> 02:59:27.656
Mm-hmm right right

02:59:27.656 --> 02:59:28.524
And now

02:59:28.524 --> 02:59:30.059
At least pre-training has also become very very clear

02:59:30.059 --> 02:59:31.594
And plus

02:59:31.594 --> 02:59:32.628
This Google

02:59:32.628 --> 02:59:33.195
Has always had

02:59:33.195 --> 02:59:36.298
This relatively strong technical background

02:59:36.298 --> 02:59:37.867
And it does things relatively systematically

02:59:37.933 --> 02:59:39.468
So I feel

02:59:39.702 --> 02:59:40.636
Pre-training at Google

02:59:40.636 --> 02:59:41.971
Is a very very controllable thing

02:59:42.004 --> 02:59:43.005
Mm-hmm predictable thing

02:59:43.005 --> 02:59:44.440
You can

