03:14:46.074 --> 03:14:48.343
You also mentioned AI is a whole system

03:14:48.576 --> 03:14:48.977
Mm-hmm

03:14:48.977 --> 03:14:50.879
What understanding do you have about how to systematically do AI

03:14:50.879 --> 03:14:53.314
Now

03:14:53.415 --> 03:14:55.283
After these two years of your work

03:14:55.283 --> 03:14:56.084
Several aspects

03:14:56.084 --> 03:14:58.987
One aspect is from the whole system perspective

03:14:58.987 --> 03:15:01.189
It needs a relatively scientific attitude

03:15:01.656 --> 03:15:03.591
That you need to clearly understand like Scaling Law

03:15:03.591 --> 03:15:04.759
You need to clearly understand

03:15:04.759 --> 03:15:06.161
What assumptions you have made

03:15:06.661 --> 03:15:08.797
And when I make a change

03:15:08.797 --> 03:15:10.732
What factors are actually related to it

03:15:10.732 --> 03:15:12.367
What factors are not related

03:15:12.367 --> 03:15:13.101
Right

03:15:13.101 --> 03:15:14.669
And this is from the organizational perspective

03:15:14.669 --> 03:15:16.271
From the people's perspective

03:15:16.271 --> 03:15:19.741
Actually requires people to be very reliable

03:15:19.741 --> 03:15:22.310
Requires very responsible people

03:15:22.377 --> 03:15:25.580
Actually every system

03:15:25.580 --> 03:15:26.781
Every evaluation framework

03:15:26.781 --> 03:15:28.416
Is very easily hacked

03:15:28.750 --> 03:15:29.951
Because you can always do something

03:15:29.951 --> 03:15:31.986
To make your metrics look very good

03:15:32.220 --> 03:15:34.722
But a trustworthy

03:15:34.722 --> 03:15:35.557
Or down-to-earth person

03:15:35.557 --> 03:15:38.326
He would actually think

03:15:38.326 --> 03:15:40.562
If the thing he did works well

03:15:40.562 --> 03:15:41.963
Is it really

03:15:41.963 --> 03:15:43.531
For example effective at large scales

03:15:43.531 --> 03:15:45.300
Did I miss some factors in between

03:15:45.467 --> 03:15:46.501
Right

03:15:46.501 --> 03:15:48.737
Actually doing things systematically

03:15:48.737 --> 03:15:50.105
Sounds like one sentence

03:15:50.105 --> 03:15:52.107
But actually doing it is very complex

03:15:52.373 --> 03:15:53.441
There are many details

03:15:53.475 --> 03:15:54.876
Many resistances

03:15:54.976 --> 03:15:57.345
It actually goes against human nature

03:15:57.479 --> 03:15:57.912
Oh

03:15:57.912 --> 03:15:59.080
Because every individual's human nature

03:15:59.080 --> 03:16:00.949
Might be to make their own things

03:16:00.949 --> 03:16:02.217
Show up better

03:16:02.517 --> 03:16:04.686
But for a company or an organization

03:16:04.686 --> 03:16:05.320
The most beneficial thing

03:16:05.320 --> 03:16:06.988
Is to make the entire company's system

03:16:07.021 --> 03:16:08.623
Very solid systematically

03:16:08.623 --> 03:16:09.224
This is actually the best for you personally

03:16:09.357 --> 03:16:10.825
Because once this system is solid

03:16:11.259 --> 03:16:12.227
You can leverage this system

03:16:13.228 --> 03:16:15.396
To produce more output

03:16:15.396 --> 03:16:16.397
But the bad thing is

03:16:16.397 --> 03:16:17.232
This system will make your individual heroism

03:16:17.232 --> 03:16:19.801
Not shine

03:16:19.801 --> 03:16:20.301
But you can rest assured that others' individual heroism

03:16:20.301 --> 03:16:25.140
Also won't shine

03:16:25.507 --> 03:16:26.841
But if you are in a system

03:16:27.242 --> 03:16:28.176
Where individual heroism can shine

03:16:28.176 --> 03:16:30.979
Then this system might

03:16:31.045 --> 03:16:35.617
Not be particularly stable

03:16:36.651 --> 03:16:39.354
Because one person leaving

03:16:39.454 --> 03:16:41.022
Might cause the entire thing to collapse

03:16:41.823 --> 03:16:45.727
For example like OpenAI

03:16:45.727 --> 03:16:48.029
You say you love to challenge difficult things

03:16:48.029 --> 03:16:50.031
But this industry seems to require

03:16:50.031 --> 03:16:51.533
Doing simple things well repeatedly

03:16:51.833 --> 03:16:53.168
Actually

03:16:53.168 --> 03:16:54.636
I think the so-called simple things

03:16:54.636 --> 03:16:56.371
Doing them well repeatedly

03:16:56.971 --> 03:16:59.407
Is actually a very difficult thing

03:16:59.407 --> 03:17:01.142
Because human nature doesn't like

03:17:01.209 --> 03:17:03.444
Doing repetitive things

03:17:03.444 --> 03:17:04.345
Because the most difficult thing in this industry

03:17:04.345 --> 03:17:06.748
Is actually doing simple things cleanly

03:17:06.748 --> 03:17:09.551
Why

03:17:10.151 --> 03:17:11.352
Because everyone can do simple things

03:17:11.352 --> 03:17:12.187
If you can't do them cleaner than others

03:17:12.187 --> 03:17:14.589
需要研究员自己对于这个系统

03:17:14.589 --> 03:17:15.223
怎么运作

03:17:15.223 --> 03:17:16.157
有一个好的理解

03:17:16.157 --> 03:17:19.627
然后以及对公司负责任才能做到

03:17:20.061 --> 03:17:22.163
否则就是你很容易做到一件事

03:17:22.163 --> 03:17:22.964
就是

03:17:23.064 --> 03:17:26.067
你可能比如说你在考虑training的时候

03:17:26.067 --> 03:17:26.868
是比别人好的

03:17:26.868 --> 03:17:28.836
但你考虑training加sampling时候比别人差

03:17:29.237 --> 03:17:31.039
你总可以选择你只是有training

03:17:31.806 --> 03:17:33.041
但这就很糟糕

03:17:33.341 --> 03:17:33.741
对

03:17:33.741 --> 03:17:35.844
所以这个就是既需要你个人的负责任

03:17:35.844 --> 03:17:39.180
又需要说组织所建立的这个体系里

03:17:39.180 --> 03:17:42.550
能够能尽量的发现这些

03:17:42.584 --> 03:17:43.785
有意的或者无意的

03:17:43.785 --> 03:17:45.486
这种边界的事情

03:17:45.486 --> 03:17:46.154
但是你作为个体的话

03:17:46.154 --> 03:17:50.258
你不知道怎么样是对全局最好的呀

03:17:50.258 --> 03:17:51.926
其实是需要

03:17:51.926 --> 03:17:53.761
我觉得如果一个研究员做不到

03:17:53.862 --> 03:17:56.331
对全局去考虑的话

03:17:57.432 --> 03:17:58.633
他就不是一个好的研究员

03:17:59.000 --> 03:18:00.068
在现在这个时代

03:18:00.835 --> 03:18:02.237
嗯 就是这个

03:18:02.237 --> 03:18:03.104
我觉得这个

03:18:03.104 --> 03:18:05.173
和你就是在学术界做research

03:18:05.173 --> 03:18:05.907
是很不一样的事

03:18:05.907 --> 03:18:06.474
哦

03:18:06.474 --> 03:18:07.475
因为在学术界做research

03:18:07.475 --> 03:18:09.077
本质上是一个人吃饱

03:18:09.077 --> 03:18:10.378
全家不愁的状态

03:18:10.578 --> 03:18:13.214
这我为我的项目负责对吧

03:18:13.514 --> 03:18:15.884
我为我的可重复性负责

03:18:16.117 --> 03:18:17.452
但是在一个公司里

03:18:17.452 --> 03:18:18.720
你其实更多的时候是

03:18:18.887 --> 03:18:20.388
我得为这个公司负责

03:18:21.289 --> 03:18:22.724
这是两种完全不一样的心态

03:18:22.924 --> 03:18:24.592
那你这种自觉性从哪里来的

03:18:26.127 --> 03:18:29.364
不知道哈哈哈哈哈

03:18:29.364 --> 03:18:32.834
我觉得我可能就是拉不下脸

03:18:32.900 --> 03:18:33.501
哈哈哈

03:18:33.568 --> 03:18:34.736
拉不下脸是什么

03:18:34.736 --> 03:18:35.403
就是

03:18:35.403 --> 03:18:36.437
你对一个公司负责任

03:18:36.437 --> 03:18:39.173
是你和这个公司的契约的一部分

03:18:39.741 --> 03:18:42.810
其实我觉得没什么道理不这么做

03:18:43.177 --> 03:18:44.812
这么做是没有原因的

03:18:47.548 --> 03:18:51.185
所以这个人英雄主义会破坏这种整体性

03:18:51.686 --> 03:18:53.087
我觉得

03:18:53.688 --> 03:18:55.390
If you're just doing it for personal heroism

03:18:55.390 --> 03:18:56.457
and acting on that basis

03:18:56.824 --> 03:18:58.459
it's very likely to undermine the bigger picture

03:18:59.060 --> 03:19:00.428
Of course, in reality you might be very capable

03:19:00.428 --> 03:19:01.663
and you actually become a hero

03:19:01.663 --> 03:19:02.497
that's also possible

03:19:04.499 --> 03:19:06.401
Since you've also been through two organizations

03:19:06.401 --> 03:19:08.703
what kind of organization do you think is better at fostering intelligence

03:19:08.903 --> 03:19:09.570
in this era

03:19:09.771 --> 03:19:12.006
I think

03:19:12.974 --> 03:19:14.008
this is actually a

03:19:15.009 --> 03:19:17.412
very controversial topic

03:19:17.478 --> 03:19:19.247
I mean

03:19:20.114 --> 03:19:20.715
as we were just discussing

03:19:20.715 --> 03:19:21.316
different organizations

03:19:21.316 --> 03:19:23.117
some tend to be more top-down

03:19:23.117 --> 03:19:24.852
some more bottom-up

03:19:24.852 --> 03:19:25.853
so the natural question is

03:19:25.853 --> 03:19:28.690
for example
which of these two types fosters more innovation

03:19:30.458 --> 03:19:31.292
The traditional view was

03:19:31.292 --> 03:19:33.561
bottom-up was a necessary condition for fostering innovation

03:19:33.961 --> 03:19:35.830
because everyone needs freedom, right

03:19:35.830 --> 03:19:37.165
only with freedom can there be innovation

03:19:37.832 --> 03:19:39.100
But purely bottom-up

03:19:39.100 --> 03:19:40.168
you find it doesn't actually work either

03:19:40.368 --> 03:19:41.069
because it just becomes chaotic

03:19:41.235 --> 03:19:42.637
That's what Google was like before

03:19:42.637 --> 03:19:43.137
Was it?

03:19:43.404 --> 03:19:44.005
Yes

03:19:44.005 --> 03:19:45.106
At least in my impression

03:19:45.106 --> 03:19:46.808
from what I understand, that's how it was

03:19:46.808 --> 03:19:47.442
It was just chaotic

03:19:47.442 --> 03:19:48.810
People didn't even know

03:19:48.810 --> 03:19:49.978
what the point of what I was doing was

03:19:50.311 --> 03:19:51.512
That might not be great either

03:19:51.512 --> 03:19:53.981
So you probably need someone

03:19:53.981 --> 03:19:55.350
or a small group

03:19:55.350 --> 03:19:57.018
who can blend these two approaches somewhat

03:19:57.085 --> 03:19:57.685
Mm-hmm

03:19:59.587 --> 03:20:00.888
That's why I think

03:20:01.522 --> 03:20:03.992
whether an organization runs well or not

03:20:04.392 --> 03:20:06.627
it looks like an organizational issue

03:20:06.627 --> 03:20:10.531
but ultimately it comes down to the tech leader

03:20:10.531 --> 03:20:11.165
Mm-hmm

03:20:11.199 --> 03:20:12.834
It's about whether this tech leader has the qualities

03:20:12.834 --> 03:20:15.403
to keep the organization running stably

03:20:16.170 --> 03:20:18.706
Because the optimal state

03:20:18.706 --> 03:20:20.341
is often the most unstable one

03:20:20.508 --> 03:20:23.177
It easily collapses toward a worse state

03:20:23.611 --> 03:20:25.980
Right, so you need a leader to control that

03:20:26.781 --> 03:20:28.716
So do you think it should always be the tech leader doing this

03:20:28.716 --> 03:20:30.251
rather than the CEO

03:20:31.853 --> 03:20:33.121
Well of course every company's CEO

03:20:33.121 --> 03:20:34.222
may have different responsibilities

03:20:34.222 --> 03:20:35.690
But there needs to be a leader

03:20:35.690 --> 03:20:37.225
I think you need at least one leader

03:20:37.225 --> 03:20:39.427
who has two qualities

03:20:40.128 --> 03:20:41.195
to be able to do this

03:20:41.863 --> 03:20:46.234
One quality is that they can fight fires themselves

03:20:47.135 --> 03:20:49.504
It's not just talking about what to do

03:20:49.504 --> 03:20:50.304
What to do

03:20:50.304 --> 03:20:52.407
but rather when something really runs into trouble

03:20:52.407 --> 03:20:54.142
they can step in and lead the team

03:20:54.142 --> 03:20:55.376
to solve the problem

03:20:56.377 --> 03:20:57.345
Of course most of the time

03:20:57.345 --> 03:20:58.346
a leader probably

03:20:58.346 --> 03:20:59.514
won't have time to do this

03:20:59.514 --> 03:21:00.815
But at least they have the capability

03:21:01.482 --> 03:21:02.450
The second important quality

03:21:02.450 --> 03:21:07.088
is that they need to understand others

03:21:07.455 --> 03:21:08.456
Even if it's something

03:21:08.456 --> 03:21:09.857
that they wouldn't do themselves

03:21:09.857 --> 03:21:11.092
they can understand

03:21:11.092 --> 03:21:12.460
why what others are doing matters

03:21:12.927 --> 03:21:13.928
They can tolerate and accommodate others

03:21:14.095 --> 03:21:15.363
That might be another quality

03:21:18.499 --> 03:21:19.233
What do you think about Google's TPU

03:21:19.233 --> 03:21:21.602
In what ways does it outperform GPUs

03:21:22.203 --> 03:21:23.137
What are its weaknesses

03:21:23.438 --> 03:21:24.639
I think

03:21:24.872 --> 03:21:26.107
From a purely hardware perspective

03:21:26.107 --> 03:21:28.976
it's hard to say which hardware is truly better or worse

03:21:28.976 --> 03:21:31.245
especially at this kind of large-scale commercial deployment

03:21:31.512 --> 03:21:32.547
Because fundamentally

03:21:32.547 --> 03:21:34.315
GPUs and TPUs

03:21:34.315 --> 03:21:35.550
In terms of usage

03:21:35.550 --> 03:21:37.452
the biggest difference, setting aside

03:21:37.452 --> 03:21:38.519
the hardware differences

03:21:38.519 --> 03:21:39.086
in terms of usage

03:21:39.086 --> 03:21:39.887
the biggest difference is

03:21:39.887 --> 03:21:41.889
GPUs have a better open-source ecosystem

03:21:42.390 --> 03:21:43.191
TPUs don't

03:21:43.324 --> 03:21:44.892
But this actually isn't an issue at large-scale commercial deployment

03:21:44.892 --> 03:21:45.893
It's not a problem

03:21:45.960 --> 03:21:47.128
Because for example, Google itself uses TPUs

03:21:47.128 --> 03:21:49.330
so naturally they'll spend time building

03:21:49.330 --> 03:21:50.765
this infrastructure

03:21:50.765 --> 03:21:52.366
And infrastructure is

03:21:52.500 --> 03:21:54.869
For example, if you're only running a thousand cards

03:21:54.869 --> 03:21:56.137
it could be a heavy burden

03:21:56.137 --> 03:21:57.972
But if you're running a cluster of hundreds of thousands of cards

03:21:57.972 --> 03:21:58.940
then building out the infrastructure

03:21:58.940 --> 03:22:00.174
isn't really that big of a deal

03:22:01.042 --> 03:22:02.643
And in practice

03:22:02.643 --> 03:22:03.711
So basically

03:22:03.711 --> 03:22:05.646
when it comes to large-scale commercial deployment

03:22:05.646 --> 03:22:07.381
neither one is inherently superior or inferior

03:22:07.515 --> 03:22:09.117
But these two do

03:22:09.317 --> 03:22:12.120
have some differences in design philosophy

03:22:12.220 --> 03:22:13.988
Take GPUs, for example

03:22:13.988 --> 03:22:16.190
At least for the more recent GPU generations

03:22:16.190 --> 03:22:17.091
I haven't used them much

03:22:17.091 --> 03:22:19.460
Like the Hopper generation of GPUs

03:22:19.560 --> 03:22:20.561
The H-series GPUs

03:22:20.561 --> 03:22:22.363
The design philosophy is that

03:22:22.363 --> 03:22:23.097
inside one pod (node)

03:22:23.097 --> 03:22:24.499
there might not be that many cards

03:22:24.499 --> 03:22:25.700
say, just eight cards

03:22:25.933 --> 03:22:27.635
and these eight cards can all interconnect with one another

03:22:27.635 --> 03:22:28.669
NVLink (NVIDIA's high-speed interconnect bus) is extremely fast

03:22:28.669 --> 03:22:30.438
So within one pod, there's basically

03:22:30.438 --> 03:22:32.373
no communication bandwidth bottleneck (insufficient bandwidth between GPUs)

03:22:33.007 --> 03:22:34.775
But TPUs take the opposite approach

03:22:34.775 --> 03:22:35.309
It means that

03:22:35.309 --> 03:22:37.979
they've abandoned pairwise interconnection between cards

03:22:38.379 --> 03:22:39.280
but they try as much as possible to

03:22:39.280 --> 03:22:40.448
fit as many cards as possible

03:22:40.448 --> 03:22:42.583
into one big rack

03:22:42.884 --> 03:22:45.186
It has this kind of

03:22:45.186 --> 03:22:47.054
3D Torus design (3D Torus topology design)

03:22:47.255 --> 03:22:48.122
So each card

03:22:48.122 --> 03:22:50.625
only connects to its three nearest neighbors in three directions

03:22:50.758 --> 03:22:52.527
but the entire cluster can be connected into one big

03:22:52.527 --> 03:22:53.060
Torus

03:22:53.327 --> 03:22:56.030
And if your compilers (compilers)

03:22:56.030 --> 03:22:57.899
or your sharding (data sharding strategy)

03:22:57.899 --> 03:23:00.434
logic is written well enough

03:23:00.434 --> 03:23:02.436
you can take advantage of this architecture

03:23:02.436 --> 03:23:04.105
Effectively speaking

03:23:04.105 --> 03:23:07.341
you get more memory capacity

03:23:07.341 --> 03:23:10.778
and also reduce a lot of communication bounds

03:23:12.713 --> 03:23:13.614
What's the downside?

03:23:14.282 --> 03:23:16.551
I think one downside is that

03:23:16.551 --> 03:23:18.953
compared to GPUs, it definitely

03:23:19.020 --> 03:23:21.222
at least at a small scale

03:23:21.222 --> 03:23:24.659
is more

03:23:24.992 --> 03:23:26.460
of a rigid structure

03:23:26.460 --> 03:23:28.729
So its ease of use

03:23:28.729 --> 03:23:30.364
or its general versatility might not be as strong

03:23:33.234 --> 03:23:35.136
Recently many neo labs have emerged in Silicon Valley

03:23:35.136 --> 03:23:36.337
What do you think of this trend?

03:23:36.404 --> 03:23:38.105
Why are they all leaving

03:23:38.139 --> 03:23:40.208
jumping ship from these big model companies

03:23:40.208 --> 03:23:41.142
to start neo labs

03:23:41.842 --> 03:23:42.410
I don't really get it

03:23:42.410 --> 03:23:45.846
Haha, my feeling is that

03:23:45.846 --> 03:23:49.250
the vast majority of neo labs will die. And

03:23:50.084 --> 03:23:53.187
Well, I think

03:23:53.187 --> 03:23:55.323
some labs genuinely have good people

03:23:55.323 --> 03:23:56.591
And some labs

03:23:56.591 --> 03:23:57.992
might actually be starting to do some real work

03:23:57.992 --> 03:23:59.026
For example, like Thinking Machines

03:23:59.026 --> 03:24:00.795
is still delivering some new things

03:24:01.162 --> 03:24:03.831
But some neo labs

03:24:04.165 --> 03:24:05.299
Please bleep out the names

03:24:05.299 --> 03:24:08.869
Haha, like XXX, that XXX

03:24:09.237 --> 03:24:11.238
I have absolutely no idea what they're trying to do

03:24:11.372 --> 03:24:11.872
And

03:24:11.872 --> 03:24:14.275
These two have actually been away from the field for a long time

03:24:15.109 --> 03:24:16.010
I think in 2026

03:24:16.010 --> 03:24:18.613
China will place a lot of emphasis on the consumer-side narrative

03:24:19.747 --> 03:24:21.482
Who becomes that super app

03:24:21.482 --> 03:24:22.216
What do you think?

03:24:22.216 --> 03:24:22.917
Do you think this

03:24:22.917 --> 03:24:25.286
It seems like nobody in Silicon Valley talks about this

03:24:25.486 --> 03:24:29.857
Right, because American enterprise is just...

03:24:29.924 --> 03:24:30.725
It's companies

03:24:30.725 --> 03:24:33.494
Or rather, the productivity software market is just too big

03:24:33.761 --> 03:24:35.896
and the profit margins are too high

03:24:36.097 --> 03:24:37.665
So for the US

03:24:37.665 --> 03:24:40.668
there was basically only ChatGPT doing consumer before

03:24:40.668 --> 03:24:41.869
and there wasn't much money in it

03:24:41.869 --> 03:24:42.670
Not much profit

03:24:43.204 --> 03:24:44.105
So

03:24:44.105 --> 03:24:46.407
now everyone will probably focus first on

03:24:46.407 --> 03:24:46.941
productivity software

03:24:46.941 --> 03:24:48.609
or enterprise

03:24:48.609 --> 03:24:49.109
And

03:24:49.176 --> 03:24:51.612
So the trends in China and the US have already diverged

03:24:51.946 --> 03:24:52.580
I think

03:24:52.713 --> 03:24:53.347
Not just AI

03:24:53.347 --> 03:24:55.883
The entire internet industry in the past was like this too

03:24:55.883 --> 03:24:56.384
It was all different

03:24:56.384 --> 03:24:58.886
What China is really strong at is the consumer side

03:24:58.886 --> 03:25:00.321
It can come up with, like

03:25:00.321 --> 03:25:03.691
really, really complex product features

03:25:03.691 --> 03:25:04.558
or structures

03:25:04.592 --> 03:25:07.361
and in a way that seems very indirect to you

03:25:07.361 --> 03:25:08.663
In a very unnatural way

03:25:09.063 --> 03:25:10.564
to snowball that profit

03:25:10.631 --> 03:25:11.432
For example

03:25:11.432 --> 03:25:12.266
What do I mean by indirect?

03:25:12.266 --> 03:25:12.933
(laughs)

03:25:12.933 --> 03:25:15.736
Like, take something like Douyin (TikTok)

03:25:16.037 --> 03:25:17.305
It's not like

03:25:17.305 --> 03:25:18.105
you watch a video

03:25:18.105 --> 03:25:20.708
and I charge you 20 cents per video, right?

03:25:21.075 --> 03:25:22.710
It says you can watch videos for free

03:25:23.144 --> 03:25:24.445
but I can quietly slip in ads

03:25:24.679 --> 03:25:25.713
I can quietly do live streaming

03:25:25.780 --> 03:25:28.115
I can quietly do e-commerce

03:25:28.382 --> 03:25:30.718
But that doesn't work for productivity software

03:25:30.718 --> 03:25:32.053
Productivity software is very straightforward

03:25:32.286 --> 03:25:34.755
Like, I help you write code

03:25:35.056 --> 03:25:36.157
My cost is 150 a month

03:25:36.157 --> 03:25:37.325
I sell it to you for 200, I make 50

03:25:38.759 --> 03:25:39.727
It's that straightforward

03:25:39.760 --> 03:25:40.161
Mm

03:25:40.161 --> 03:25:43.364
Yeah, I think what the US has shown in the past

03:25:43.364 --> 03:25:45.066
is that with these very straightforward products

03:25:45.232 --> 03:25:46.701
it can push technology to the extreme

03:25:47.368 --> 03:25:48.669
But there's never been

03:25:48.669 --> 03:25:51.439
a product that felt so sophisticated

03:25:51.439 --> 03:25:53.708
that you can't live without it

03:25:54.642 --> 03:25:56.310
yet you don't feel like it's taking your money

03:25:56.477 --> 03:25:57.745
but it's actually making money from you

03:26:01.315 --> 03:26:03.851
Hearing you say that, I suddenly feel Meta should just copy ByteDance

03:26:05.052 --> 03:26:06.754
Yeah, but I don't think Meta is as strong as ByteDance

03:26:06.821 --> 03:26:09.290
Because Meta can't find its own niche either

03:26:09.290 --> 03:26:10.157
And

03:26:10.157 --> 03:26:13.494
there's no American company doing this

03:26:13.494 --> 03:26:15.463
No one has found the niche that Doubao occupies

03:26:15.463 --> 03:26:17.098
Then Meta should just copy Doubao

03:26:17.098 --> 03:26:19.600
It doesn't need such strong model capabilities either

03:26:21.202 --> 03:26:23.337
But I still think the Americans making products

03:26:23.337 --> 03:26:26.774
fundamentally, the people doing consumer products aren't good enough

03:26:26.774 --> 03:26:28.109
Far behind China

03:26:29.210 --> 03:26:31.178
This is the accumulation of the past decade, right?

03:26:31.379 --> 03:26:32.179
Yeah

03:26:32.480 --> 03:26:33.180
Mm

03:26:33.180 --> 03:26:36.250
Because the positive feedback loop in the US over the past decade

03:26:36.250 --> 03:26:37.985
all came from doing B2B

03:26:37.985 --> 03:26:39.920
A lot of enterprise stuff

03:26:39.954 --> 03:26:41.956
Or it's just too easy to make money in the US

03:26:42.156 --> 03:26:42.656
Mm

03:26:42.656 --> 03:26:43.691
When it's too easy to make money

03:26:43.691 --> 03:26:45.760
you won't rack your brains over how to make money

03:26:46.160 --> 03:26:46.560
Hey

03:26:46.560 --> 03:26:48.462
Haven't a lot of people come to chat with you?

03:26:48.796 --> 03:26:50.064
Any interesting people?

03:26:51.899 --> 03:26:53.634
Oh, well
A lot of people from China came

03:26:53.634 --> 03:26:54.301
Tech companies

03:26:54.769 --> 03:26:58.406
I think they're all pretty interesting

03:26:58.406 --> 03:27:01.509
And I did find that Chinese people doing products

03:27:01.509 --> 03:27:04.745
probably think in more sophisticated ways

03:27:04.745 --> 03:27:05.780
More sophisticated

03:27:05.780 --> 03:27:07.815
Yeah, they think more...

03:27:07.815 --> 03:27:09.583
Their thought process is more convoluted

03:27:09.583 --> 03:27:11.852
Yeah, it's a completely different style from the US

03:27:11.852 --> 03:27:12.820
America is like

03:27:12.820 --> 03:27:14.155
As I just said about America

03:27:14.155 --> 03:27:15.256
It's like

03:27:15.589 --> 03:27:16.891
you build something and sell it directly

03:27:16.991 --> 03:27:18.025
Yeah, it's simple

03:27:18.025 --> 03:27:19.126
That's how it is

03:27:20.661 --> 03:27:22.630
You just need this capability

03:27:23.130 --> 03:27:25.633
Once you have it, you just need to be cheaper than others

03:27:25.833 --> 03:27:27.034
Then I can earn more than you

03:27:28.035 --> 03:27:29.069
And you can't do anything about it

03:27:29.303 --> 03:27:30.104
Okay

03:27:30.638 --> 03:27:31.405
What about China?

03:27:31.405 --> 03:27:32.873
China seems to be all about this pattern

03:27:32.873 --> 03:27:34.809
Not making money at first

03:27:35.209 --> 03:27:36.277
But once it starts making money

03:27:36.277 --> 03:27:37.945
you can't stop it

03:27:37.945 --> 03:27:38.779
It's just that

03:27:38.779 --> 03:27:40.314
it can really form that

03:27:40.314 --> 03:27:41.782
that self-sustaining

03:27:41.782 --> 03:27:42.716
that loop

03:27:43.150 --> 03:27:44.552
When it really gets that flywheel spinning

03:27:44.552 --> 03:27:46.053
you can't break in anymore

03:27:47.421 --> 03:27:48.122
Do you think American companies

03:27:48.122 --> 03:27:49.557
understand ByteDance now?

03:27:51.826 --> 03:27:52.827
My feeling is no

03:27:52.960 --> 03:27:53.761
Not yet

03:27:54.328 --> 03:27:55.196
It's already so big

03:27:56.330 --> 03:27:58.132
Oh, you mean whether they take it seriously?

03:27:58.132 --> 03:27:59.066
Of course they do

03:27:59.133 --> 03:28:00.167
Everyone definitely knows

03:28:00.167 --> 03:28:02.336
ByteDance is a severely undervalued

03:28:02.603 --> 03:28:04.004
In terms of its valuation

03:28:04.004 --> 03:28:05.573
It's a severely undervalued company

03:28:05.573 --> 03:28:07.174
I think that's very clear to everyone

03:28:07.508 --> 03:28:09.376
And

03:28:09.610 --> 03:28:10.511
I think it's also clear

03:28:10.511 --> 03:28:12.746
that in the consumer market

03:28:12.746 --> 03:28:13.848
On this end, I actually think

03:28:13.848 --> 03:28:17.017
No American company can compete with ByteDance

03:28:18.018 --> 03:28:20.821
But after all it's a Chinese company

03:28:22.323 --> 03:28:24.458
At least in terms of public perception

03:28:24.458 --> 03:28:25.659
After all it's a Chinese company

03:28:27.595 --> 03:28:28.929
So do people understand it

03:28:29.296 --> 03:28:30.898
I don't think people understand it

03:28:31.031 --> 03:28:31.999
But look at Meta

03:28:31.999 --> 03:28:34.034
It's also actively poaching people from ByteDance

03:28:34.535 --> 03:28:37.338
Mm-hmm, do you have any idols in the AI industry

03:28:37.605 --> 03:28:38.739
Or people you admire

03:28:40.007 --> 03:28:41.709
Although you've been in the AI industry for a short time

03:28:42.209 --> 03:28:43.310
No no no, nothing

03:28:43.310 --> 03:28:44.411
I just feel

03:28:47.147 --> 03:28:49.416
When I came to this industry

03:28:49.416 --> 03:28:51.685
The era of individual heroism had already passed

03:28:52.086 --> 03:28:53.354
So there are no heroes

03:28:54.588 --> 03:28:56.690
Sometimes you even think old-era heroes are a bit stupid

03:28:57.157 --> 03:28:58.525
Ah, right

03:28:58.525 --> 03:29:00.528
So really there's nothing

03:29:01.295 --> 03:29:02.429
Who do you think is quite stupid

03:29:03.297 --> 03:29:04.265
Let's not talk about this

03:29:04.265 --> 03:29:08.202
No comment hahahaha

03:29:08.969 --> 03:29:10.804
Right, I think it's

03:29:10.804 --> 03:29:11.572
Different from doing physics

03:29:11.572 --> 03:29:12.172
I think when doing physics

03:29:12.172 --> 03:29:13.874
There were still some

03:29:13.874 --> 03:29:17.244
People I think really much smarter than me

03:29:17.578 --> 03:29:18.312
Like me

03:29:18.312 --> 03:29:20.080
When I was doing my PhD my young advisor was

03:29:20.080 --> 03:29:21.849
I think he, Douglas Stanford

03:29:21.849 --> 03:29:24.251
I think he's just much smarter than me

03:29:24.251 --> 03:29:25.119
I think he

03:29:26.520 --> 03:29:27.288
Maybe also seeing him

03:29:27.288 --> 03:29:29.223
Made me feel in that field

03:29:29.223 --> 03:29:30.457
Not very useful

03:29:30.457 --> 03:29:31.458
With him around what do they need me for

03:29:31.458 --> 03:29:32.993
Right, haha

03:29:33.193 --> 03:29:34.762
You came to AI to do a dimensionality reduction attack right

03:29:34.828 --> 03:29:35.563
Not a dimensionality reduction attack

03:29:35.563 --> 03:29:36.931
But anyway it feels like AI this thing

03:29:36.931 --> 03:29:38.899
Doesn't really need brains

03:29:40.100 --> 03:29:41.268
Doesn't really need brains

03:29:41.268 --> 03:29:42.036
Really doesn't need brains

03:29:42.036 --> 03:29:42.803
Then what does it need

03:29:43.037 --> 03:29:43.871
I think this

03:29:43.871 --> 03:29:46.273
The most important trait in this industry

03:29:46.273 --> 03:29:47.875
Is being reliable

03:29:48.442 --> 03:29:50.044
Doing things carefully

03:29:50.044 --> 03:29:51.946
And being responsible for what you do

03:29:51.946 --> 03:29:53.247
This is the most important trait

03:29:53.647 --> 03:29:58.652
You say how much brains those things need, I think

03:30:00.888 --> 03:30:03.524
They're all things undergraduates can do

03:30:04.258 --> 03:30:06.493
But you say AI has no individual heroism

03:30:06.493 --> 03:30:09.196
Now an AI researcher is priced so high

03:30:09.196 --> 03:30:10.497
Like a star player transfer

03:30:10.965 --> 03:30:12.533
I don't know if it's a good thing or bad thing

03:30:13.200 --> 03:30:13.934
For me personally

03:30:13.934 --> 03:30:15.069
Of course I'm very happy

03:30:15.269 --> 03:30:16.870
I benefit from this

03:30:16.870 --> 03:30:18.672
Right hehehe

03:30:18.672 --> 03:30:20.874
But um

03:30:20.874 --> 03:30:21.475
Actually speaking

03:30:21.475 --> 03:30:23.077
I don't know if this thing

03:30:24.111 --> 03:30:24.912
Is a good thing

03:30:25.079 --> 03:30:26.747
Why do you think the price has become so high

03:30:27.715 --> 03:30:29.917
I think maybe on one hand

03:30:29.917 --> 03:30:31.619
Everyone thinks this thing is scarce

03:30:32.086 --> 03:30:34.888
But actually it might not be that scarce

03:30:34.888 --> 03:30:36.757
Because training a person

03:30:36.757 --> 03:30:37.825
Although this thing isn't that hard

03:30:37.825 --> 03:30:39.893
But training a person requires an environment

03:30:39.960 --> 03:30:42.763
You need to have that opportunity to be exposed to this thing

03:30:42.763 --> 03:30:43.998
To learn this thing

03:30:44.164 --> 03:30:44.999
Without that opportunity

03:30:44.999 --> 03:30:46.400
No matter how smart you are it's useless

03:30:46.767 --> 03:30:49.803
Maybe in the past people who could encounter that opportunity

03:30:49.803 --> 03:30:50.738
Weren't that many

03:30:51.338 --> 03:30:54.274
So in the market it might be relatively scarce

03:30:54.274 --> 03:30:54.975
From this perspective

03:30:54.975 --> 03:30:55.542
Mm-hmm

03:30:56.010 --> 03:30:57.678
But I think another aspect is also

03:30:58.379 --> 03:31:00.347
Maybe the hype about people is a bit excessive

03:31:01.015 --> 03:31:01.815
Right

03:31:02.416 --> 03:31:03.684
Really like to mythologize individuals

03:31:03.684 --> 03:31:04.118
Now

03:31:04.118 --> 03:31:05.252
Right I think

03:31:06.220 --> 03:31:07.021
Really

03:31:08.622 --> 03:31:10.124
Just say it again

03:31:10.324 --> 03:31:12.826
This is a collectivist thing haha

03:31:13.227 --> 03:31:15.562
Then many people are also very curious

03:31:15.629 --> 03:31:17.965
Because

03:31:17.965 --> 03:31:21.435
Maybe many companies also want to recruit AI people

03:31:21.535 --> 03:31:24.138
Then you think the most important thing is still being reliable

03:31:24.238 --> 03:31:25.673
What metrics are there for this

03:31:25.673 --> 03:31:28.208
How can you quickly judge whether a person is reliable

03:31:28.275 --> 03:31:29.176
Whether they do things carefully

03:31:29.443 --> 03:31:33.981
Everyone has some methods they use to measure

03:31:33.981 --> 03:31:36.216
I of course also have some of my own tricks

03:31:36.383 --> 03:31:37.451
It's just that I

03:31:37.551 --> 03:31:40.721
I used to design an interview question

03:31:41.388 --> 03:31:42.990
Let me briefly explain it

03:31:42.990 --> 03:31:43.624
This

03:31:43.624 --> 03:31:44.391
It shouldnt be confidential

03:31:44.391 --> 03:31:45.592
So I should be able to talk about it

03:31:45.659 --> 03:31:46.026
Um

03:31:46.026 --> 03:31:47.027
So the interview question is actually quite simple

03:31:47.027 --> 03:31:50.931
I need this person to, within 24 hours,

03:31:51.465 --> 03:31:55.202
complete a reinforcement learning project

03:31:55.202 --> 03:31:56.804
from scratch

03:31:57.237 --> 03:31:59.573
They have to choose on their own

03:31:59.573 --> 03:32:00.207
what kind of model

03:32:00.207 --> 03:32:01.975
I tell them what resources are available

03:32:01.975 --> 03:32:03.777
and they choose what model to use

03:32:03.777 --> 03:32:04.645
what data to use

03:32:04.645 --> 03:32:05.979
what algorithm to use

03:32:05.979 --> 03:32:07.214
and train the model

03:32:07.347 --> 03:32:08.282
Within 24 hours

03:32:08.282 --> 03:32:10.751
I give them 24 hours to get this done

03:32:11.051 --> 03:32:12.219
And after the 24 hours are up

03:32:12.219 --> 03:32:14.488
they'll have a one-hour discussion with me

03:32:15.022 --> 03:32:16.356
So this thing

03:32:16.356 --> 03:32:19.093
isn't that hard in the AI era

03:32:19.093 --> 03:32:20.027
Without AI

03:32:20.027 --> 03:32:20.561
this would be impossible

03:32:20.561 --> 03:32:22.362
No one could do it in 24 hours

03:32:22.362 --> 03:32:23.697
But with AI, it's actually quite easy

03:32:23.697 --> 03:32:25.199
Because AI can do the whole thing for you

03:32:25.532 --> 03:32:26.600
But why still do this?

03:32:26.600 --> 03:32:27.568
There are two reasons

03:32:27.568 --> 03:32:28.268
There are many reasons

03:32:28.268 --> 03:32:29.470
Among them

03:32:29.470 --> 03:32:31.071
Two reasons why it was designed this way

03:32:31.071 --> 03:32:34.274
One reason is that I think in this era, evaluating someone

03:32:34.341 --> 03:32:35.609
evaluating someone

03:32:35.609 --> 03:32:37.010
like whether they write good code

03:32:37.678 --> 03:32:38.545
is actually useless

03:32:38.946 --> 03:32:41.081
Because most people don't need to write code themselves anymore

03:32:41.882 --> 03:32:44.284
What's more important is

03:32:44.284 --> 03:32:46.086
whether they can effectively leverage AI

03:32:46.220 --> 03:32:48.589
So that's one aspect of evaluating this

03:32:48.822 --> 03:32:50.824
The second aspect is that there's a trap here

03:32:51.158 --> 03:32:53.160
If you let AI do everything

03:32:53.293 --> 03:32:54.895
but you don't really try to understand

03:32:54.895 --> 03:32:56.130
what AI did for you

03:32:56.330 --> 03:32:58.699
you'll be exposed during that one-hour discussion

03:32:59.399 --> 03:33:00.134
That's a

03:33:00.134 --> 03:33:01.869
That's where people fail

03:33:02.269 --> 03:33:04.104
So the other thing this tests

03:33:04.104 --> 03:33:06.373
is whether you've truly formed a collaboration with AI

03:33:06.373 --> 03:33:08.108
Or if you just completely handed it off

03:33:08.542 --> 03:33:11.278
That's something I personally value very much

03:33:11.278 --> 03:33:12.312
That also

03:33:12.312 --> 03:33:13.280
reflects whether this person

03:33:13.280 --> 03:33:15.582
is someone reliable

03:33:15.983 --> 03:33:17.151
Of course, this

03:33:17.151 --> 03:33:18.218
The design of this question itself

03:33:18.218 --> 03:33:21.355
also has some rather dark cleverness to it

03:33:21.355 --> 03:33:23.190
Like why it was designed as 24 hours

03:33:23.957 --> 03:33:26.927
is to see how much this person values this opportunity

03:33:27.194 --> 03:33:27.928
Can they stay up all night

03:33:27.995 --> 03:33:29.096
Right, hahaha

03:33:29.096 --> 03:33:30.330
If they're willing to pull an all-nighter

03:33:30.497 --> 03:33:32.432
they can survive these 24 hours

03:33:32.566 --> 03:33:34.034
If they can't make it

03:33:34.034 --> 03:33:34.535
then it just means

03:33:34.535 --> 03:33:36.837
they probably don't value this opportunity that much

03:33:36.904 --> 03:33:37.704
Haha

03:33:39.006 --> 03:33:40.741
So for people younger than you

03:33:40.741 --> 03:33:42.342
Do you think AI is still

03:33:42.342 --> 03:33:45.212
a blue ocean

03:33:45.212 --> 03:33:46.313
a place with lots of opportunities

03:33:46.313 --> 03:33:48.248
I think purely working on language models

03:33:48.248 --> 03:33:49.850
